// StdlibSource — what a compile knows about the STDLIB SOURCE it loaded, as opposed to the user's own
// code. Two facts, derived once per compile from one walk of the merged Maxon module:
//
//   1. WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS THIS PROGRAM CANNOT REACH — code dead-function elimination will drop
//      before the back end, so no pre-elimination pass may let it decide anything about the program.
//   2. WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS NEED THE x64-WINDOWS SUBSTRATE, and which runtime entry each one reaches —
//      the fact a user's own call site is refused with, so a target rejection names the stdlib function
//      THEY wrote instead of a line inside `stdlib/`.
//
// ⭐ **NOTHING HERE DEPENDS ON *WHICH* STDLIB FILES ARE LOADED — only on which functions came from
// stdlib.** shv2 loads a growing subset of `stdlib/` today and all of it eventually; both facts above get
// MORE important in that end state, not less, because a program built on `Map`, `String` and `FilePath`
// crosses into stdlib on nearly every line, and a diagnostic positioned inside one of them is a
// diagnostic about a file the user never opened.
//
// So the question asked throughout is **"is this function STDLIB source or USER source?"**, answered from
// the function's own source path (`isStdlibFunction`) — never from a registry of loaded modules. Grow the
// set of loaded files to every file under `stdlib/` and every function in this file still answers
// correctly, over a larger set, with no edit.

// ⭐ **WHICH FILES ARE STDLIB IS ESTABLISHED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY RE-TESTING PATHS.** The stdlib loader
// enumerates `project.stdlibDir` and records every file it parsed from there in `project.stdlibSourcePaths`
// — so "this file lies under the checkout's `stdlib/`" is already known of exactly those files, and of no
// others. That record is what the facts below are derived from.
//
// ⚠ **THE ALTERNATIVE WAS MEASURED AND IS EXPENSIVE.** Re-deriving it by asking `FilePath.isInside` of
// each of the program's source paths costs about FOUR String allocations per question — on Windows
// `canonicalForCompare` clones and lower-cases both sides, and the needle is an interpolation — so it puts
// a term into every compile that grows with the number of files the user wrote. Measured on the scale
// ladder: +60 allocations at rung 0 rising to +1,920 at rung 5, doubling with the corpus, for a fact the
// loader already had. Asking it per FUNCTION instead of per file was worse again (+3,633 rising to
// +48,273). `isStdlibFunction` answers the per-function form from a set built once.
//
// ⭐ **IS THIS FUNCTION STDLIB SOURCE?** A `ByteArray` hash against that set. This is the question every
// pass asks, and it stays true as stdlib grows from one module to all of it — nothing here depends on
// WHICH files those are, only on their having come from `stdlib/`.
export function isStdlibFunction(project Project, func IrFunction) returns bool
	return project.stdlibFacts.stdlibFunctions.contains(func.name)
end 'isStdlibFunction'

// The directory name walked for, and a generous bound on how far up the tree to look. A checkout is
// shallow (`<root>/maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2.exe` is three levels under the `stdlib/` that sits at
// `<root>`), so 64 is far more than enough while still terminating on a pathological path rather than
// looping forever.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED BECAUSE IT IS ALSO THE STDLIB'S MODULE DIRECTORY (N1c)** — `Queries.namespaceAnchorFor`
// anchors a stdlib file at this directory's PARENT, so every stdlib file's module directory is this name
// or a descendant of it. `Project.isStdlibModuleDirectory` is the one reader, and it reads this rather
// than a second `"stdlib"` literal so a rename cannot leave the two disagreeing about where the library
// layer is.
export let StdlibDirName = "stdlib"
let MaxStdlibSearchDepth = 64

// Locate the checkout's `stdlib/` by walking UP from the COMPILER's own executable directory.
//
// ⚠ NOT from `Directory.currentPath()` (v1's `findStdlibPath`): the spec runner and `run_program`
// compile a program in a throwaway temp dir, so the working directory has no `stdlib/` above it.
// The compiler binary always lives inside the checkout, so its own path is the reliable anchor —
// the same reason the bootstrap's `FindStdlibPath` (0-Compiler.cs) walks up from
// `AppContext.BaseDirectory`. A failure to locate it is a hard, LOUD error rather than a silent
// skip: every compile loads stdlib source, so a missing stdlib is a real environment problem the
// user must see, not a `Clock.nowMs()` that mysteriously reports E3004.
export function locateStdlibDir() returns FilePath throws CompileError
	let exePath = try Process.executablePath() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	let exeDir = try exePath.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	return try stdlibDirAbove(exeDir)
end 'locateStdlibDir'

// The same walk, from a directory the CALLER names — which is a different question from the one above
// and has one other asker: `TreeLock` needs the checkout containing the path a command WRITES INTO
// (the project directory, the spec directory, the scale corpus), not the one the compiler's own
// sources came from. Those two answers are the same file in every ordinary invocation and are not
// required to be: the walk is what both of them mean by "the checkout", so it is written once.
//
// It THROWS rather than answering "none" for `locateStdlibDir`'s reason there; `TreeLock` reads the
// throw as "no checkout above this path", which is a real and ordinary answer for a program compiled
// in a temp directory.
export function stdlibDirAbove(start FilePath) returns FilePath throws CompileError
	var dir = start

	var depth = 0
	while depth < MaxStdlibSearchDepth 'search'
		// Interpolated so the argument is a committed `String`: a bare module-`let` literal stays
		// untyped and matches BOTH `FilePath.join` overloads (String / FilePath) — an E3007 ambiguity —
		// whereas a String VALUE selects `join(String)` cleanly.
		let candidate = dir.join("{StdlibDirName}")
		if Directory.exists(candidate) 'found'
			return candidate
		end 'found'
		// `parent()` throws at the filesystem root — the correct place for the search to give up.
		dir = try dir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
		depth = depth + 1
	end 'search'

	throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
end 'stdlibDirAbove'

// A set of function names, for O(1) membership. Used for both "unreachable from `main`" and "declared in
// stdlib source", exactly like `DestructorSet`.
export typealias FunctionNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐⭐ **THE BODY DEMAND IS PER FILE AND HAS TWO STATES — all of this file's bodies, or none of them.**
// A user file always builds. A stdlib file builds once ANY function it declares is reached, and then it
// builds ALL of them.
//
// ⚠ **PER FILE RATHER THAN PER FUNCTION, AND BOTH REASONS ARE LOAD-BEARING:**
//
//   • **CORRECTNESS.** A per-function demand has to be asked in the parser, mid-declaration, of a name —
//     and there are TWO spellings of a function's name there. `sourceName` is what the source wrote
//     (`Math.exp`); `overloadRegistrationNameFor` may mangle it (`FilePath.join#FilePath`), and it is the
//     MANGLED one that reaches `IrFunction.name` and therefore the reachability walk's answer. Asking with
//     the wrong one skips a body the program calls, and the call then lands on the bare `retVoid`
//     `skipUnreachedFunctionBody` left — a SILENT WRONG ANSWER, in exactly the family
//     `deriveStdlibFacts` already warns about ("it runs BEFORE overload resolution, so a callee name here
//     is provisional"). A per-FILE demand never asks about a name at all.
//   • **COST.** The demand is discovered round by round, so a per-function demand re-parses a file once per
//     function of it that is reached — and, because a memo key built from the whole-program demand moves
//     for EVERY file whenever ANY name is added, it re-parses all twenty-one of them each round. MEASURED
//     before this was per-file: `Math.exp(0.0)` spent **125.9 ms in `parse`** against 54.2 ms for the
//     unmodified compiler — a REGRESSION on the programs the change exists to speed up.
//
// The cost of the coarser unit is building the unreached bodies of a file that is already being used, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination` drops those exactly as it dropped them before.
// (There is no typealias for it: `typealias X = bool` is not legal Maxon, and a one-word alias for a
// primitive would be a name to keep in step rather than a fact to state. The header above is the fact.)

// A stdlib function name -> the x64-windows-only runtime entry its body reaches, directly or through
// other stdlib functions. Membership IS "this function needs the Win32 substrate", so the map is its own
// mark column and there is no second set to keep in step with it.
export typealias StdlibSubstrateEntryMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArray)

// Stdlib function name -> its DENSE node index in the substrate fixpoint's call graph. A distinct alias
// from `FuncIndexByName`, whose values are merged-MODULE function indices: these index the far
// smaller stdlib-only key space `buildCsr` is handed.
typealias StdlibNodeByName = Map with (ByteArray, CsrIndex)

// ⭐ THE TWO FACTS ABOUT STDLIB SOURCE A COMPILE DERIVES, from ONE walk of the merged module. They are one
// object rather than two `Project` fields because they are one derivation over one call graph:
// `substrateEntries` is meaningful only for the functions `unreachable` does NOT hold (code the program
// cannot reach is invisible to it, substrate and all), and two fields assigned from two calls could come
// to disagree about which functions those are.
export type StdlibFacts
	// Every function the merged module got from stdlib source — the per-function form of `isStdlibPath`,
	// materialized once so the question can be asked of every function of the program without paying a
	// path comparison each time (see `isStdlibFunction`).
	export let stdlibFunctions as FunctionNameSet
	// Stdlib functions NOT reachable from `main`. Dead-function elimination drops them before the back
	// end, so a pass that runs earlier and examines function BODIES to decide something about the PROGRAM
	// — the runtime floor it carries (`scanRuntimeUsage`), the targets it can be built for
	// (`checkCalls`) — must not let them decide it, or the program pays for code it does not contain.
	// User functions are never in it: a diagnostic in unreached USER code is still the user's bug, while
	// a runtime requirement of unreached STDLIB code is nobody's.
	export let unreachable as FunctionNameSet
	// The REACHABLE stdlib functions that need the x64-windows substrate, and which runtime entry each
	// one reaches. Read at a call site that crosses from user code into stdlib, to refuse the program on
	// a target that cannot lower it — at the user's own span.
	export let substrateEntries as StdlibSubstrateEntryMap

	export static function create(stdlibFunctions FunctionNameSet, unreachable FunctionNameSet, substrateEntries StdlibSubstrateEntryMap) returns StdlibFacts
		return Self{stdlibFunctions: stdlibFunctions, unreachable: unreachable, substrateEntries: substrateEntries}
	end 'create'

	// A compile that has not derived them yet, and the answer for a compile that loaded no stdlib source
	// at all: no function is stdlib, nothing is unreachable-stdlib and nothing needs the substrate.
	export static function empty() returns StdlibFacts
		return Self.create(FunctionNameSet.create(), unreachable: FunctionNameSet.create(), substrateEntries: StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create())
	end 'empty'
end 'StdlibFacts'

// ⭐ DERIVE BOTH FACTS. Runs once per compile, right after the module is merged and before any pass that
// reads either of them — and before `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`, which is the earliest
// reader (`Compiler.compileToCodeResult`).
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES `signatures` FOR ONE THING: THE FUNCTIONS A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CALLS.** Those calls are
// made by `__module_init`, which does not exist yet at this point and is called by the entry STUB rather
// than from `main` once it does — so neither the walk below nor its short-circuit can see the edge in any
// body. It is read off the DECLARATION that causes the call instead (`ProgramSignatures.globalInitCallees`),
// which is also what lets this run before the synthesis: see that function for why the answer does not
// depend on the global liveness this derivation is about to decide.
export function deriveStdlibFacts(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns StdlibFacts throws CompileError
	// A prior diagnostic leaves the merged module and its registries describing a program that is already
	// refused, so deriving anything from it can only be noise — the refusal the four siblings around this
	// call already make (`checkConformance`, `checkWhereConstraints`, `checkTypeSymbolNamespace`,
	// `checkGenericAliasCycles`), and this was the one pre-pipeline derivation without it. Nothing that runs
	// before the pipeline's error gate READS these facts to report anything: `scanRuntimeUsage` only sets
	// usage bits, and the two passes that do report off them (`checkCalls`'s target refusal,
	// `insertRangeChecks`) are inside the pipeline, downstream of that gate.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedModule'
		return StdlibFacts.empty()
	end 'malformedModule'

	let stdlibNames = try stdlibFunctionNames(project)
	if stdlibNames.count() == 0 'noStdlibSource'
		return StdlibFacts.empty()
	end 'noStdlibSource'

	var stdlibSet = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in stdlibNames 'seed'
		stdlibSet.insert(name)
	end 'seed'

	// Built once, here, and handed to both walks below: the short-circuit and the precise walk ask
	// different questions of the same list, and a second build would be the same arena walked twice.
	let initCallees = signatures.globalInitCallees()

	// ⭐ COMMON-CASE SHORT-CIRCUIT. If no op in USER code REACHES stdlib, no stdlib function is
	// reachable at all, so ALL of them are unreachable and NONE can be blamed for a substrate — reached by
	// ONE allocation-free O(ops) scan, WITHOUT the O(functions) name->index map and reached-array the
	// from-`main` walk allocates, and without the substrate fixpoint. That map is the whole per-compile
	// memory cost of loading stdlib source into a program that never touches it, so skipping it keeps the
	// cost a per-compile CONSTANT instead of growing with the user program. It stays EXACT: a stdlib
	// function referenced by nothing is reachable from nothing.
	if not userCodeReachesStdlib(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees) 'noneReferenced'
		var allUnreachable = FunctionNameSet.create()
		for name in stdlibNames 'markAll'
			allUnreachable.insert(name)
		end 'markAll'
		return StdlibFacts.create(stdlibSet, unreachable: allUnreachable, substrateEntries: StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create())
	end 'noneReferenced'

	// User code DOES call stdlib — resolve exactly which stdlib functions the program REACHES from `main`
	// and mark only the untouched ones. This precise walk (and its maps) runs ONLY for a program that
	// actually uses stdlib.
	let reachable = try reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees)
	var unreachable = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in stdlibNames 'classify'
		if not reachable.contains(name) 'unreached'
			unreachable.insert(name)
		end 'unreached'
	end 'classify'

	return StdlibFacts.create(stdlibSet, unreachable: unreachable, substrateEntries: stdlibSubstrateEntries(module, stdlibNames: stdlibNames, unreachable: unreachable))
end 'deriveStdlibFacts'

// ⭐⭐ **GROW THE BODY DEMAND BY ONE ROUND — the fixpoint step `queryAllModule` iterates.**
//
// It asks the SAME question `deriveStdlibFacts` asks, of the same walk, and that is deliberate rather
// than convenient: the set of bodies this compile BUILDS and the set `lowerMaxonToStd` LOWERS must be
// decided by one derivation, or a body could be lowered that was never parsed. Round 0 merges every
// file with no stdlib body built, so the walk sees the user's own edges into stdlib and stops there;
// each later round has the bodies the previous one demanded, so the walk goes one call deeper. It
// terminates because the demand only ever grows and is bounded by the stdlib functions that exist.
//
// ⚠ **A ROUND THAT ADDS NOTHING IS THE COMMON CASE, AND IT IS THE FIRST ONE.** A program whose user
// code names no stdlib function at all — most of `specs-shv2` — settles here, having built not one
// stdlib body. That is exactly `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s short-circuit reached one tier earlier.
//
// Returns whether the demand actually grew, which is the caller's loop condition.
export function growStdlibBodyDemand(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns bool throws CompileError
	// A prior diagnostic leaves the merged module describing a program that is already refused, so a
	// reachability walk over it can only be noise — `deriveStdlibFacts` declines for the same reason, one
	// screen down, and a compile that stops here simply builds no further bodies.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedModule'
		return false
	end 'malformedModule'

	let stdlibNames = try stdlibFunctionNames(project)
	if stdlibNames.count() == 0 'noStdlibSource'
		return false
	end 'noStdlibSource'

	var stdlibSet = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in stdlibNames 'seed'
		stdlibSet.insert(name)
	end 'seed'

	let initCallees = signatures.globalInitCallees()
	// The same short-circuit `deriveStdlibFacts` opens with, and it is the whole saving: nothing this
	// program reaches — no op in its code, and no root on either list — names a stdlib function, so no
	// stdlib body can be reached and the walk's O(functions) name->index map is never built.
	if not userCodeReachesStdlib(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees) 'noneReferenced'
		return false
	end 'noneReferenced'

	let reachable = try reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees)

	// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS RECORDED AS A SET OF FILES, NOT OF NAMES, AND THAT IS WHAT SURVIVES THE MEMO
	// DROP.** `queryAllModule` clears `parseCache` before it re-folds, so nothing may ask a later round
	// "which functions does this file declare?" — the artifact that knew is gone. A file recorded here is
	// still recorded then. (Measured the other way round: keyed on names and read back through the parse
	// cache, every file answered "declares nothing reached" after the drop, every body stayed skipped, and
	// `lowerStdToX64` panicked on the `retVoid` of a function the program calls.)
	var grew = false
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for fileIndex in 0 upto project.stdlibSourcePaths.count() 'eachFile'
		let demandedThisFile = try demandOneStdlibFileIfReached(project, path: try project.stdlibSourcePaths.get(fileIndex) otherwise panic("growStdlibBodyDemand: fileIndex {fileIndex} is below project.stdlibSourcePaths.count()"), reachable: reachable)
		grew = grew or demandedThisFile
	end 'eachFile'

	return grew
end 'growStdlibBodyDemand'

// Every function the merged module got from stdlib source, in load order. Read back from each stdlib
// file's PARSE ARTIFACT — a memo HIT, since `queryAllModule` has already parsed it — so the cost is
// O(stdlib functions) and touches nothing the user wrote. A `ByteArrayArray` rather than a set because
// the names are unique by construction (E3006 rejects a duplicate) and the substrate fixpoint needs a
// Demand ONE stdlib file's bodies if any function it declares is reachable, answering whether the demand
// set grew. ⭐ **THE PATH ARRIVES AS A PARAMETER**, which is what lets it be named three times: read
// inline out of `project.stdlibSourcePaths` it is a borrow of `project`, and `queryParseOps` and the
// `insert` both WRITE `project`, so the caller would need the same `try … get(fileIndex) otherwise
// panic(…)` spelled once per use — one fact, three copies. Same cure as
// `Parser.conformanceRequiresMethod`.
function demandOneStdlibFileIfReached(project Project, path FilePath, reachable FunctionNameSet) returns bool throws CompileError
	if project.db.demandedStdlibBodyFiles.contains(path) 'alreadyBuilding'
		return false
	end 'alreadyBuilding'

	let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: path)
	for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not reachable.contains(func.name) 'notReached'
			continue
		end 'notReached'

		project.db.demandedStdlibBodyFiles.insert(path)
		return true
	end 'eachFunc'

	return false
end 'demandOneStdlibFileIfReached'

// dense ORDER for its node space as well as membership.
function stdlibFunctionNames(project Project) returns ByteArrayArray throws CompileError
	var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for fileIndex in 0 upto project.stdlibSourcePaths.count() 'eachFile'
		let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: try project.stdlibSourcePaths.get(fileIndex) otherwise panic("stdlibFunctionNames: fileIndex {fileIndex} is below project.stdlibSourcePaths.count()"))
		for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
			names.push(func.name)
		end 'eachFunc'
	end 'eachFile'
	return names
end 'stdlibFunctionNames'

// The reachable-from-`main` closure over the merged Maxon module, as a set of function NAMES.
// The Maxon-tier twin of `DeadFunctionElimination.markCalleeOf`: the FOUR ops that name a callee
// are edges — `call`, `tryCall`, `functionRef` (a TAKEN ADDRESS is a static edge even though the
// call consuming it is indirect) and `asyncCall`. Every other variant names no static callee.
//
// A callee that is not a module function — an as-yet-uninstalled `__gt_*`/`__clock_*` runtime
// helper, which is added only during/after lowering — is simply not queued, exactly as
// `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction` handles the same case.
//
// ⛔⛔ **"REACHABILITY BY DIRECT `call`/`funcAddr` EDGES IS EXACT FOR STDLIB SOURCE ... BECAUSE NO STDLIB
// FUNCTION IS REACHED BY A WITNESS DISPATCH OR AN `.rdata` RELOC" IS WHAT THIS PARAGRAPH SAID, AND W35
// MADE IT FALSE.** That premise held only while `print` was a compiler-recognized BARE NAME rather than
// `stdlib/Print.maxon`'s declaration. The moment it became ordinary stdlib source, an interface method
// implementation that prints put a stdlib function behind a DATA edge — and
// `specs-shv2/interface-dispatch.md`'s `dispatch-void-method` (a `Thing.show()` whose body is
// `print("{label}\n")`, reached only through `__witness_Thing.Printer`) went from passing to a COMPILER
// PANIC: `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead: 'print' is in StdlibFacts.unreachable`. The guard was right
// and the walk was wrong, which is what a guard stating a premise is for.
//
// ⇒ **THE WITNESS EDGE IS CREDITED AT THE CONFORMER THIS WALK REACHES** — `markConformerWitnessImpls`,
// below, which carries the whole argument and the two cuts that were wrong before it. ⚠ This paragraph
// named a `markWitnessTableImpls` keyed on the DISPATCH OP for one commit, and that function is gone: it
// covered one of the three `ensureWitnessTable` routes and left the other two panicking on programs the
// merge base compiles. **If you are reading a description of the design here rather than at the function,
// distrust it** — this is the copy that rots.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE CONFORMER KEY RESTS ON TWO PREMISES, NOT ONE, AND ONLY THE FIRST IS SELF-EVIDENT. BOTH WERE
// PROBED AT THE W35 REVIEW.**
//
//   • **P1 — a value of a user-declared type `T` implies a reached function OF `T`.** This is E3076, and it
//     is enforced: `Thing{tag: 1}` written outside `Thing` is *"type 'Thing' can only be constructed from
//     within its own methods"*. The value kinds that could evade it are the ones that are not built by a call —
//     an ENUM case, a generic INSTANCE — and both are REFUSED at the only place a table gets built, the
//     widening: `enum Step implements Coded` with a method-carrying `Coded` is `E3005 … type 'Step' does
//     not implement interface 'Coded'`, and `Box with ExitCode` into a `Printer` place is the same refusal.
//     An `Error` enum DOES widen, and `Error` has zero requirements, so there is no slot to credit.
//     ⇒ P1 holds today because the conformers that can reach a table are exactly the ones E3076 governs.
//
//   • **P2 — a BUILTIN conformer's requirement impls are never stdlib SOURCE. IT HAS EXPIRED TWICE AND IS NO
//     LONGER A PREMISE AT ALL; the seed below is what stands in its place.** The conformer key structurally
//     CANNOT credit a builtin: no `String.<method>` or `int.<method>` module function is ever reached, since
//     those calls lower to `__str_*`/inline ops. MEASURED that builtin conformers really do reach tables, and
//     not only through the container route `markConformerWitnessImpls`'s last paragraph covers:
//     `type Keyed uses T where T is Hashable` instantiated at `String` and at `int` compiles and runs
//     (exit 14 = `9 + 5`), building `__witness_String.Hashable` and `__witness_int.Hashable`. That was
//     harmless only while `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` SYNTHESIZED every such impl. ⇒ **The day a LISTED
//     module declares `extension <builtin> implements <Iface>` with a corpus body, this key misses it** —
//     W17 was that day for `String`/`Character`, and the `stdlib/Array.maxon:637` case this paragraph named
//     in advance (`extension Array implements Hashable, Equatable where Element is Hashable and Equatable`)
//     is the second. Both are carried by `conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods`, whose membership rule and
//     loudness argument live at that function.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THIS IS WHY A RESIDUAL HERE IS TOLERABLE: AN UNDER-APPROXIMATION IS LOUD, ALWAYS.** The same
// walk decides both what is BUILT (`growStdlibBodyDemand`) and what is filed `unreachable`, so the two can
// never disagree in the silent direction — a function this walk misses is unbuilt AND in that set, and
// `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panics the moment elimination reaches it through the reloc. The
// SILENT failure — filed reachable, body never built, an empty function the program calls — is a different
// mistake, it was the `witnessSlotImpls` cut, and this key cannot make it. Treat a panic from that guard as
// this premise list having acquired a third entry, and add it here.
//
// ⚠ **A NARROWER GATE EXISTS AND IS NAMED RATHER THAN DENIED — it was not taken, and the reason is not
// that it is unsound.** `ensureWitnessTable`'s `interfaceName` argument is ALWAYS statically known: a
// declared interface-typed parameter, a declared interface RETURN, a `where T is I` constraint, or the
// container key/element's `Hashable`/`Equatable`. Gating on the PAIR — a reached conformer `T` AND an
// interface the program can statically name — would drop the over-approximation
// `markConformerWitnessImpls`'s last paragraph measures (a `FilePath` program crediting four impls it never
// boxes) to nothing. It was left alone because it re-introduces predicting the CALL SITES, which is the shape the
// measured `+81%` cut got wrong, for +1.1% allocations and zero emitted bytes; and because the `extends`
// case needs care (a `Base`-typed place against `T implements Derived` builds `__witness_T.Base`, so the
// filter must walk `extends` rather than test set membership). A rung, with the ladder read, not a review edit.
//
// ⭐ The name index is the shared, CHECKED one (`FunctionNameIndex`, A1w). It used to resolve one
// last-wins and unchecked, walking ONE of two same-named bodies and crediting the callees of whichever it
// picked; a walk that silently under-approximates reachability is not something to leave resting on a
// tie-break nobody stated.
//
// ⚠ **THIS SITE NOW RUNS BEFORE `installManagedGlobalInit` (S2h), SO IT CAN NO LONGER MEET A REAL
// DUPLICATE** — a `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declaration of `__module_init`/`__maxon_global_cleanup` against
// the compiler's own has no compiler side yet. That refusal did not move, it merely stopped being made
// TWICE: `PassPipeline.run`'s index sees the identical pair one screen below, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination.markCompilerOwnedRoot` catches the case with no pair to count. See
// `FunctionNameIndex`, which is where what each site can see is written down.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ROOT SET IS `main` PLUS EVERY FUNCTION A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CALLS, and the second half is
// not a widening — it is the same reachability with an edge no BODY carries.** `__module_init` makes those
// calls, and it is synthesized after this walk and called by the entry STUB, so it is invisible here in
// both directions. `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots` roots it unconditionally (its kind 2), so leaving
// the edge out is not conservative: the two derivations disagree, `lowerMaxonToStd` lowers no body for a
// stdlib factory named only by an initializer, and the elimination walk reaches the empty function it
// left. MEASURED before the fix, on `var sep = FilePath.separator()`: a `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`
// panic on a program with nothing wrong with it (`specs-shv2/top-level-factory-globals.md`'s
// `stdlib-factory-reached-from-the-initializer`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND IT RUNS BEFORE OVERLOAD RESOLUTION, SO A CALLEE NAME HERE IS PROVISIONAL — WHICH IS WHY THE
// OVERLOAD SET IS CREDITED WHOLE.** `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` rewrites `call FilePath.join`
// into `call FilePath.join#FilePath` — and its own header opens *"OVERLOAD RESOLUTION RUNS FIRST, BEFORE
// ANYTHING READS A CALLEE"* — but it runs inside `PassPipeline.run`, and `deriveStdlibFacts` runs one
// screen ABOVE that call in `compileToCodeResult`. So this walk is the one reader that precedes it, and
// the bare name it sees is the registration name of the FIRST member only.
//
// MEASURED, and it is a compiler PANIC on a correct program, not a size regression:
// `specs/filepath.md`'s `filepath-join-filepath` writes `base.join(child)` against
// `stdlib/FilePath.maxon:357/371`'s two `join` overloads. The walk credited `FilePath.join` (the `String`
// member, declared first), filed `FilePath.join#FilePath` as unreachable, `lowerMaxonToStd` skipped its
// body — and then dead-function elimination, which runs AFTER resolution and sees the real callee,
// reached it and tripped `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`.
//
// ⇒ A provisional callee names a SET, and this walk answers "might this be reached?", so every member is
// credited. That is exact for the question asked and cannot make the assert fire: an OVER-approximation
// only ever removes names from `unreachable`, and every consumer of that set (`lowerMaxonToStd`,
// `insertRangeChecks`, `scanRuntimeUsage`, `checkCalls`) treats absence as "compile it normally".
// ⚠ Moving `resolveOverloadedCalls` above this instead was considered and REJECTED: it sits after
// `resolveTypes` today, so hoisting it would hand overload selection the UNRESOLVED parameter spellings
// and could change which member a call picks — trading a loud panic for a silent misdispatch.
//
// The unused member is still dropped from the emitted image: dead-function elimination runs after
// resolution, keys on the real callee, and prunes it there. What this costs is only that
// `scanRuntimeUsage` may read an unreached sibling overload's body — and only in a program that already
// calls that stdlib name.
function reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet, initCallees ByteArrayArray) returns FunctionNameSet throws CompileError
	let byName = (try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project)).byName

	var reached = BoolArray.create()
	reached.resize(module.funcCount())
	var worklist = FuncIndexArray.create()
	var names = FunctionNameSet.create()

	markReachable(UserEntryName.toByteArray(), project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	for callee in initCallees 'eachInitializerRoot'
		markReachable(callee, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachInitializerRoot'

	// ⭐⭐ **A LAZY STATIC ACCESSED FROM A USER FUNCTION NOTHING CALLS IS STILL BUILT, SO ITS INITIALIZER'S
	// CALLEES ARE SEEDED FROM EVERY USER BODY AND NOT ONLY FROM THE REACHED ONES (W115).** This asymmetry is
	// not this walk's invention — it is `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`', stated in its own header:
	// *"A USER function is still credited whether or not anything calls it … but a stdlib function
	// `StdlibFacts.unreachable` names is skipped"*. That is the rule that decides whether the slot EXISTS,
	// so it is the rule that decides whether `installManagedGlobalInit` synthesizes a `__lazy_init$` calling
	// these bodies — and a root missing under it is `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`, not a lost prune.
	// Matching it exactly is what keeps the two derivations from disagreeing; the reached-code half is
	// `markLazyStaticInitCallees`, which covers the stdlib bodies this seed deliberately does not.
	seedLazyStaticInitCalleesOfUserCode(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **A WITNESS IMPL IS CREDITED FOR A PAIR — A CONFORMER THE WALK HAS EVIDENCE OF, AND AN INTERFACE
	// THE REACHED CODE CAN NAME (W188).** Both halves were already here and each was asked ALONE:
	// `markConformerWitnessImpls` credited every conformance a reached type DECLARES (evidence about the
	// type, none about the interface), and the corpus seed credited every conformance the six roster
	// conformers declare (evidence about the interface's existence, none about which one). Neither is wrong;
	// together they are a cross product, and it is the CROSS that is exact — `ensureWitnessTable` is called
	// with a conformer AND an interfaceName, and its `interfaceName` is always statically known.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE COST OF ASKING THEM SEPARATELY, MEASURED (W188):** with `int`/`float`/`bool` on the roster, a
	// program whose only constraint is `Hashable` seeded `int.hash`, then the conformer key saw a REACHED
	// `int.*` and credited every other `int`/`float` impl — including `Stringable`'s `return "{self}"`, whose
	// float arm drags `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s decimal bignum cone. **Image 12,117 → 21,333 bytes (+76%),
	// emitted CODE byte-identical at 2,783**: 91 `.rdata` panic literals for bodies the program cannot call.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A FIXPOINT BECAUSE BOTH COORDINATES GROW DURING THE WALK**, and either one growing owes the
	// other's whole list: a newly NAMED interface must be crossed with every conformer already reached, and a
	// newly REACHED conformer with every interface already named. Both sets only grow and both are bounded by
	// what the program declares, so it terminates.
	// ⚠ **BUILT ONCE PER WALK, NOT ONCE PER REACHED FUNCTION.** `markEveryDeclaredWitnessImplOf` asks whether
	// a conformer is the seed's, and it is asked of EVERY qualified function the drain reaches — so a roster
	// rebuilt at the ask is a seven-push array plus its doubling reallocations per reached stdlib function.
	// That is `Parser.arraySurfaceMemberNames`' measured lesson (**+1,023 allocations at rung 0 rising to
	// +26,009 at rung 5** when its roster was per-call), and the same shape is refused here in advance.
	let seedConformers = conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods(signatures)
	var witnessInterfaces = InterfaceNameSet.create()
	var witnessInterfaceOrder = ByteArrayArray.create()
	var interfacesSeeded = 0 as FieldIndex
	while true 'seedRounds'
		drainReachableWorklist(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, seedConformers: seedConformers, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names, witnessInterfaces: witnessInterfaces, witnessInterfaceOrder: witnessInterfaceOrder)
		if interfacesSeeded == witnessInterfaceOrder.count() 'noInterfaceLeftToSeed'
			break
		end 'noInterfaceLeftToSeed'

		while interfacesSeeded < witnessInterfaceOrder.count() 'eachNewlyNamedInterface'
			let interfaceName = try witnessInterfaceOrder.get(interfacesSeeded) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: witnessInterfaceOrder.get OOB at {interfacesSeeded} despite the loop guard")
			interfacesSeeded = interfacesSeeded + 1
			for conformerName in seedConformers 'eachSeededConformer'
				markWitnessImplsOfConformer(conformerName, interfaceName: interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			end 'eachSeededConformer'
		end 'eachNewlyNamedInterface'
	end 'seedRounds'

	return names
end 'reachableMaxonFunctionNames'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE CONFORMERS THE CONFORMER KEY STRUCTURALLY CANNOT REACH — the seed's membership rule, in one
// place, because the omission of a name from it is the whole defect this list exists to repair.**
// `markConformerWitnessImpls` credits a type's requirement impls once the walk reaches ANY function OF that
// type, which is exact for a type E3076 governs (P1). It is EMPTY for a type the COMPILER MINTS VALUES OF: a
// `String` and a `Character` are born from literals and every operation on one lowers to `__str_*`, and an
// `Array` is born from an array literal or `Array.create()` and every operation on one lowers to `__managed_*` —
// MEASURED, on `Key.create()` + `k.push(1)` + `k.count()` with `stdlib/Array.maxon` listed, the emitted module
// holds `@main` and `@print` and NO `Array.<method>` at all. So no reached function is ever qualified by one
// of these names and the key has nothing to fire on.
//
// ⚠ **MEMBERSHIP IS TWO CLAUSES AND BOTH ARE NARROWING, so "it is a builtin" is neither of them.**
//
//   • **The compiler mints its values without a call of its own** — as above. `Map` is a builtin container
//     and is deliberately ABSENT on this clause: a dictionary literal lowers to `Map.create` + `Map.upsert`
//     (`Parser.emitContainerLiteralCreate`), ordinary reached calls, so the conformer key fires for it. MEASURED —
//     a `where T is Iterable` program instantiated at `Map with (Num, Num)` emits `@Map.createIterator` and
//     compiles clean with `Map` on no seed.
//   • **A LISTED module declares its conformance**, so a witness slot can name a CORPUS body. This is what
//     keeps `int`/`float`/`bool`/`Set` off the list although they satisfy the first clause: their impls are
//     `BuiltinConformanceRuntime`'s synthesized ones, and a synthesized body is not stdlib source, so there
//     is nothing `unreachable` could hold and nothing to seed. The day one of them acquires a listed
//     declaring module is the day it joins — by the same route `Array` did, below.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THAT DAY CAME FOR `int`, `float` AND `bool` (W188): `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon` IS LISTED,
// AND ITS `extension int implements Hashable, Equatable, Comparable, Stringable, Cloneable` IS THE LISTED
// DECLARING MODULE THE CLAUSE ABOVE NAMES.** `undefinedImplNames` therefore stops synthesizing
// `int.hash`/`int.equals`/`int.compare` (and their `float`/`bool` siblings) and a witness slot's `funcAbs64`
// reloc names the CORPUS body instead — a DATA edge this Maxon-tier walk cannot see.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED, BEFORE THE THREE LINES BELOW EXISTED, THE MOMENT THE MODULE WAS LISTED: 124
// `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panics** — `int.hash` (83), `int.equals` (23), `int.compare` (18) —
// which is `String`/`Character`'s 130 at W17 and `Array.hash`'s panic at ARR5b, a third time, on the roster
// this function IS. **The omission is loud by construction, which is the whole reason a roster is tolerable
// here**, and the previous two entries were each found by exactly this panic naming the missing name.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`Vector` JOINED AT `W189` AND LEFT AT `W190`, AND THE ENTRY AND ITS REMOVAL ARE ONE ARGUMENT READ IN
// TWO DIRECTIONS.** It was admitted as the first clause in its purest form — **NOT ONE of its members was
// ever reached**, because every one of them was compiler-served (`Parser.vectorSurfaceMemberNames`,
// `gateAndEmitContainerCreate`), so the conformer key had nothing whatever to fire on. **W190 falsified
// exactly that premise**: `create`, `count`, `get` and `set` are `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s now, so any program
// holding a vector reaches a `Vector.<method>` and the conformer key fires for it like any other type's —
// which is the ONE clause `Map`'s deliberate absence rests on, one container over.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED at W189, before the members retired: `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panicking on
// `ArrayIterator.current`** — the cascade one step past the witness slot, since the corpus `createIterator`
// returns an `ArrayIterator` whose own `Iterator` table is reached from there. **RE-MEASURED at W190 with the
// line struck: the suite is green**, which is what says the key now reaches that cascade by the ordinary
// road. An omission here is loud by construction (see the ⭐ block below), so the removal is checkable in the
// one direction that matters.
//
// ⚠ **THE THREE PRIMITIVE ENTRIES ARE THE BARE NAMES AND NEED NO `arrayRecordBaseName` TREATMENT**, because a
// primitive's spelling cannot be contested: `int`/`float`/`bool` are KEYWORDS, so no user `type int` exists
// to shadow the corpus declaration into `__int`. That is the same uncontestability `String` and `Character`
// rest on (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`); only the array name moves.
//
// ⭐ **AN OMISSION HERE IS LOUD, ALWAYS, WHICH IS WHY A ROSTER IS TOLERABLE WHERE A PREDICTION WOULD NOT BE.**
// This walk decides both what is BUILT and what is filed `unreachable`, so a conformer missing from this list
// is a function `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` PANICS on BY NAME the moment a
// witness slot reaches it. That is how `Array` was found: `markConformerWitnessImpls`' header had predicted it
// in so many words — *"the day a LISTED module declares `extension <builtin> implements <Iface>` with a corpus
// body, this key misses it … `stdlib/Array.maxon:637` … listing it is the change that would need this
// revisited"* — and listing the module produced exactly that panic on `Array.hash`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ARRAY ENTRY IS THE CORPUS DECLARATION'S *COMPILED* NAME AND NOT THE BARE ONE (ARR5b).** This
// seed names the qualifier a corpus body is registered under, and under a contest that is the mint
// (`__Array.hash`, `Parser.shadowRenamedTokens`): the bare `Array` there is the USER's own container, which
// declares no witness impl and so seeds nothing — leaving exactly the omission the paragraph above says
// PANICS BY NAME. `arrayRecordBaseName` is the corpus declaration as the stdlib spells it, which is what
// this walk reads. The other two names are uncontestable (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`), so
// only this one moves.
function conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods(signatures ProgramSignatures) returns ByteArrayArray
	var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
	names.push(StringConformerName)
	names.push(CharacterConformerName)
	names.push(signatures.arrayRecordBaseName())
	names.push(IntConformerName)
	names.push(FloatConformerName)
	names.push(BoolConformerName)
	return names
end 'conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods'

// Drain the worklist to a fixpoint, answering whether anything it reached DEMANDS a witness table. Split out
// of `reachableMaxonFunctionNames` because the walk runs twice — once to find the demand, once for the
// bodies the seed adds — and a second copy of the drain is a second place for a marking to be added to one
// side only, which is the exact defect this rung repairs one screen down.
// ⚠ **THE COST GUARD THAT STOOD HERE WENT WITH THE BOOL (W188).** It skipped the
// `witnessConstraintsOfMethod` ask once the answer was known, on the ground that *"once the answer is known
// it cannot change, and `or` is monotone"*. A SET of names is not monotone in that sense — the interface a
// later function names is one the seed still owes — so every reached function is asked. The ask is a
// qualified-name split and a handful of map lookups, and it is now the only thing standing between a
// `Hashable` program and 9 KB of `.rdata` it cannot reach.
function drainReachableWorklist(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, seedConformers ByteArrayArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet, witnessInterfaces InterfaceNameSet, witnessInterfaceOrder ByteArrayArray)
	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("drainReachableWorklist: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		let func = module.getFunc(idx)
		markConformerWitnessImpls(func, project: project, signatures: signatures, seedConformers: seedConformers, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		markMaxonCallees(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, func: func, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		collectWitnessPositionInterfaces(func, project: project, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: project.funcSignatures, into: witnessInterfaces, order: witnessInterfaceOrder)
	end 'walk'
end 'drainReachableWorklist'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE PREMISE THIS WALK'S HEADER CALLED `P2` HAS EXPIRED, AND THIS IS ITS REPLACEMENT (W17).**
// That premise reads: *"a BUILTIN conformer's requirement impls are never stdlib SOURCE … It is harmless
// ONLY because `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` SYNTHESIZES those impls, so nothing behind those tables is stdlib
// source."* It then names exactly what would end it — *"The day a LISTED module declares … with a corpus
// body, this key misses it"* — and W17 is that day: `stdlib/String.maxon` and `stdlib/Character.maxon` are
// listed, and each DEFINES the very `hash`/`equals`/`compare` the runtime used to synthesize. The installer
// builds an impl only for a name the module does not already define (`undefinedImplNames`), so the witness
// slot's `funcAbs64` reloc now names a CORPUS body.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED, the moment those modules were listed: **130 `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panics** —
// `Character.hash` (87), `String.hash` (40), `String.equals` (2), `Character.compare` (1). The guard was
// right and the walk was wrong, which is what a guard stating a premise is for, and it is the SECOND time
// this walk has been corrected by it (W35 was the first).
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE CONFORMER KEY CANNOT REACH THESE, AND SO WHY A SEED IS NEEDED AT ALL.**
// `markConformerWitnessImpls` credits a type's requirement impls once the walk reaches ANY function OF that
// type — sound for a user type, because E3076 makes a value of it impossible without one. A `String` value
// is born from a LITERAL and every operation on it lowers to `__str_*`, so **no `String.<method>` module
// function is ever reached** and the key has nothing to fire on. The table is still built, because
// `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` says these two conform with no `implements` at all.
//
// ⛔⛔ **W17 MADE THE SEED UNCONDITIONAL, AND THAT BROKE THE RUNTIME FLOOR FOR EVERY PROGRAM IN THE
// LANGUAGE (repaired here).** With the cone listed those five names ARE stdlib names, so the root list made
// `anyRootNamesStdlib` answer TRUE for a program whose whole text is `return 0 as ExitCode`: the
// allocation-free short-circuit never fired again, the precise walk ran on every compile, and it reached
// `Character.hash` — whose conformer key then credits `Character.clone`/`Character.init`, bodies that build
// the managed record `Character{managed: …}`. `scanRuntimeUsage` turned the heap on,
// `Compiler.compileToCodeResult` laid down `MmRuntime.runtimeGlobals()`, and five
// `static-variables/data-section-*` pins read their `var` 24 bytes off its true offset. MEASURED, on that
// program: `data { __slab_cursor@0  __slab_end@8  __mm_alloc_count@16 }` where the section must be EMPTY —
// `compileToCodeResult`'s own invariant, *"a `return 42` program allocates nothing, so it gets no allocator,
// no `.data` slots and no leak check"*.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **SO THE SEED IS GATED ON THE QUESTION IT IS THE ANSWER TO: CAN THE REACHED CODE NAME A WITNESS
// TABLE?** Every table in the emitted image is registered by `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable`, and it has
// exactly TWO routes in. This asks for the evidence each route needs, of REACHED code only — never for a
// prediction of which call sites will fire, which is the shape the measured `+81%` cut got wrong (see
// `markConformerWitnessImpls`):
//
//   • **A `where T is I` CONSTRAINT witness** (`materializeWitnessArg`), carried by every call to a method of
//     a `where`-constrained type. Its evidence is that method's own DECLARATION, and the reached method is
//     exactly the one whose call materialized the table.
//   • **An EXISTENTIAL WIDENING at a call argument or a return** (`witnessForExistentialValue`). Same shape:
//     the widening happens at the CALLER, but it happens because the CALLEE declares an interface-typed
//     parameter or return — and a callee that is called is a callee this walk reaches.
//
// ⚠ **THERE WAS A THIRD, AND IT IS THE ONE WHOSE EVIDENCE COULD ONLY BE AN OP: a COMPILER-OWNED runtime
// callee that threads a CONTAINER's key witnesses as ordinary arguments** — the `__set_*`/`__map_*` keys until
// W105, then `__cs_contains`'s hardcoded `Character` until W129. Both retirements are the same move: the
// corpus replacement is a declared generic method, so its witnesses are a DECLARATION the first route already
// covers. A new compiler-owned witness-carrying callee needs that route back, in `markMaxonCallees`, because
// nothing declares such a body's parameters.
//
// ⇒ **EVERY ROUTE'S EVIDENCE IS A REACHED DECLARATION, so the demand is discovered BY the
// drain and the drain is simply run twice** — see `reachableMaxonFunctionNames`. The seed stays an
// OVER-APPROXIMATION in the CONFORMER (a program that widens only an `int`, or keys a `Set` by one, still
// credits both byte records), and that is the safe direction by this walk's own rule: *"an OVER-approximation
// only ever removes names from `unreachable`, and every consumer of that set treats absence as 'compile it
// normally'."* The narrower gate — reading the receiver instance's element type off the value columns, as
// the lowering itself does — is available and was not taken: it re-derives lowering's conformer computation
// at the Maxon tier, and an UNDER-approximation there is an empty body the back end calls.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THE OBLIGATION W17 DID NOT DISCHARGE IS DISCHARGED HERE.** `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s header
// warns that a root kind which is *"a marking pass"* can be added to one side only — W17 added one, so a
// program whose user code names no stdlib function took the short-circuit, filed every stdlib name
// `unreachable` INCLUDING these, and `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panicked on the witness slot
// (MEASURED with the cone listed: 33 cases, every one a `Map`/`Set` with `String` keys or a `String`
// conformance — `let m = ["a": 1]` panicked on `String.hash`, while the same program with one `print` line
// added compiled, because the `print` dragged it onto the precise path). The two sides now ask ONE
// PREDICATE — `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition` — and the short-circuit's
// exactness argument carries it unchanged: if user code names no stdlib callee then NO stdlib function is
// reachable, so the reached set is a subset of user code, and a user function is the only place a
// demand can be. (An op-borne predicate stood beside it until W129, under the same argument.)
//
// ⭐ **THIS HALF IS THE DECLARATION-BORNE PAIR**, asked of the CALLEE rather than of the call site, because
// the callee is what this walk holds and a caller's demand implies it:
//
//   • An interface-typed PARAMETER or RETURN: `appendExistentialWitnessArgs` materializes a table for a
//     concrete argument crossing into the parameter, and `lowerReturn` for one crossing into the return. A
//     struct FIELD of interface type needs no arm of its own — a value reaches one only through a
//     constructor or a setter, which is a call with an interface-typed parameter.
//   • A `where` CONSTRAINT the method carries: `appendWitnessArgs` materializes one table per constraint at
//     every call to it. Asked through `witnessConstraintsOfMethod` — `LowerMaxonToStd.witnessParamCountForCallee`'s
//     own source — so the list this gate tests and the list that pass fills are one answer, and a conditional
//     TYPE EXTENSION's extra constraints (which no `structWhereConstraints` read would see) are covered by
//     construction.
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT ANSWERS WITH THE INTERFACE NAMES, NOT WITH A BOOL (W188).** It was
// `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`, a yes/no that seeded EVERY method of EVERY conformance each roster
// conformer declares. That was free while the roster was `String`/`Character`/`Array`, whose conformances
// are `Hashable`/`Equatable`/`Comparable` — bodies with no literals. **It stopped being free the moment
// `int`/`float`/`bool` joined it**: their `Stringable` impl is `return "{self}"`, and a `float`'s
// interpolation drags `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s entire decimal bignum cone (`__bigMulSmall`,
// `__bigShiftLeft`, `__float_bitsFromText`, …) behind it.
//
// ⛔ **MEASURED, on a program that names `Hashable` and no other interface and touches no primitive:
// image 12,117 → 21,333 bytes (+9,216, +76%), with the emitted CODE byte-identical at 2,783** — the whole
// growth is `.rdata`: **91 panic literals** none of that program's code can reach. A `String`-keyed twin
// grew identically (13,335 → 22,551). `DeadFunctionElimination` drops the BODIES, and nothing drops the
// literals a lowered body registered, which is `W129`'s recorded finding one seed over.
//
// ⇒ **The seed is now keyed on the interfaces the reached code can actually NAME**, which is the narrower
// gate this walk's own header describes and declines: *“`ensureWitnessTable`'s `interfaceName` argument is
// ALWAYS statically known”*. The over-approximation that remains is the CONFORMER (a `Hashable` program
// still credits `int.hash` and `String.hash` alike), which is the cheap half — those are the bodies with no
// literals.
//
// ⚠ **UNDER-APPROXIMATING HERE IS A PANIC, NOT A SILENT WRONG ANSWER**, and the seed loop in
// `reachableMaxonFunctionNames` is what keeps it exact: a name discovered by a LATER round re-seeds, so a
// witness position reached only through a seeded body is still covered.
function collectWitnessPositionInterfaces(func IrFunction, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, into InterfaceNameSet, order ByteArrayArray)
	for paramType in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
		if maxonTypeTag(paramType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialParam'
			noteWitnessPositionInterface(paramType, project: project, into: into, order: order)
		end 'existentialParam'
	end 'eachParam'

	match func.maxonReturnType 'returnPosition'
		void then break 'returnPosition'
		value(inner) then noteWitnessPositionInterfaceIfExistential(inner, project: project, into: into, order: order)
	end 'returnPosition'

	for constraint in witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction(func, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: funcSignatures) 'eachConstraint'
		noteInterfaceName(constraint.interfaceName, into: into, order: order)
	end 'eachConstraint'
end 'collectWitnessPositionInterfaces'

// The interface an `interfaceRef` position names, filed once. The ids of a MERGED module's signature types
// are project-global (`mergeArtifact` remaps them), so the project's interner is the one they live in.
function noteWitnessPositionInterface(t MaxonType, project Project, into InterfaceNameSet, order ByteArrayArray)
	noteInterfaceName(interfaceNameOfType(t, typeNames: project.typeNames), into: into, order: order)
end 'noteWitnessPositionInterface'

// The RETURN arm's form: a return type is a `MaxonReturnType`, so the tag test lives past the `value` arm
// rather than in front of it — `maxonReturnTypeTag` panics on `void` by design.
function noteWitnessPositionInterfaceIfExistential(t MaxonType, project Project, into InterfaceNameSet, order ByteArrayArray)
	if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
		return
	end 'notAnExistential'

	noteWitnessPositionInterface(t, project: project, into: into, order: order)
end 'noteWitnessPositionInterfaceIfExistential'

// ⭐ **THE SHORT-CIRCUIT SCAN'S HALF OF THE PAIR — THE SAME THREE SOURCES, ASKED AS A YES/NO.**
// `userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable` walks EVERY user function before the demand walk runs and needs no names,
// so it must not pay for a set and an array per function. The two readers stay one answer by construction:
// every source below is one of the collector's, and neither may grow a fourth alone (W17's measured defect
// is exactly a source added to one side).
function functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition(func IrFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap) returns bool
	for paramType in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
		if maxonTypeTag(paramType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialParam'
			return true
		end 'existentialParam'
	end 'eachParam'

	if not maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(func.maxonReturnType) and maxonReturnTypeTag(func.maxonReturnType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialReturn'
		return true
	end 'existentialReturn'

	return witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction(func, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: funcSignatures).count() > 0
end 'functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition'

// ⚠ **A SET FOR MEMBERSHIP AND AN ARRAY FOR ORDER, because the seed loop must be able to WALK the names
// it has not yet acted on** — and it must walk them in a fixed order, so what a compile builds cannot depend
// on a hash bucket.
function noteInterfaceName(name ByteArray, into InterfaceNameSet, order ByteArrayArray)
	if into.contains(name) 'alreadyNamed'
		return
	end 'alreadyNamed'

	into.insert(name)
	order.push(name)
end 'noteInterfaceName'

// The `where`-clause half of the collector above, kept whole because its two-branch read of the signature
// map is `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`'s own and its reason is unchanged — see the paragraphs above.
function witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction(func IrFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap) returns WhereConstraintArray

	// ⭐ **THE EXTENSION HALF IS READ OFF THE REGISTRATION NAME'S OWN SIGNATURE (ARR3b)**, because the
	// declaration-sweep map is keyed by the DECLARED name and a D7 overload member does not wear it — see
	// `FuncSignature.extensionConstraints`. A function with no recorded signature (a synthesized graph, a
	// witness adapter) has no registration name to read one off, so it falls back to the DECLARATION-SWEEP
	// clause — which is exactly what every callee read before ARR3b, and cannot be wrong for a name no
	// declaration wears. Over-answering here costs a walk; under-answering skips a table the image needs.
	if let sig = try funcSignatures.get(func.name) 'declared'
		return signatures.witnessConstraintsOfMethod(func.name, extensionConstraints: sig.extensionConstraints)
	end 'declared'

	return signatures.witnessConstraintsOfMethod(func.name, extensionConstraints: signatures.extensionMethodConstraintsOf(func.name))
end 'witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction'

// Mark `name` reachable and queue its body. A name no module function carries (a runtime helper,
// or a root whose runtime this program never installs) is not found and not queued — nothing to
// walk. Mirrors `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction`.
function markReachable(name ByteArray, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	markOneReachable(name, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

	// ⭐⭐ **A MINTED PARAMETER-DEFAULT HELPER IS PROVISIONAL FOR THE SAME REASON THE BASE CALLEE BESIDE IT
	// IS, AND ONLY THE BASE WAS WIDENED (W81).** The two names are emitted by one act: filling a short call
	// appends `f(x, m: __paramDefault#f#2())`, both spellings taken from the sweep's by-name registry, and
	// `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` then re-points BOTH — the call at `commitOverloadChoice`, the
	// helper at `repointDefaultedArgHelpers`, at the resolved member's own
	// `__paramDefault#f#<member suffix>#2`. So a helper name standing in this walk denotes a SET exactly as
	// its base does, and it is handled here rather than in a second reader for that reason.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE MISS WAS SILENT FOR EVERY OVERLOAD SET WHOSE FIRST MEMBER HAPPENS TO DEFAULT SOMETHING, WHICH
	// IS WHY IT SURVIVED W74.** The first declaration of a name KEEPS its source spelling
	// (`Parser.declaredParamDefaultHelperName`), so ITS helper is named by the swept name and this walk found
	// it; every later member's helper is named by the member's mangled registration name and nothing marked
	// it. MEASURED at `stdlib/Testing.maxon`'s listing, on three programs differing only in one argument's
	// type: `Expect.equal(5, expected: 5)` compiles (the `AssertedInt` arm is declared first), while
	// `Expect.equal(true, expected: true)` and `Expect.equal("x", expected: "x")` are
	// `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panics naming that member's own helper — the two derivations
	// disagreeing over an ordinary CALL edge, with no data edge anywhere near it. Supplying the argument
	// (`message: "m"`) compiles either way, since then no helper is minted at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SHORT-CIRCUIT SIDE OWES NOTHING HERE, and that is an argument rather than an omission.**
	// `nameReachesStdlib` asks whether user code crosses into stdlib at all, and a helper call is only ever
	// emitted as an ARGUMENT of the call whose parameter it defaults — the same function, the same block. So
	// the base callee's own op is always present for that scan to answer TRUE on, by whichever of its two
	// clauses applies, and a helper name can never be a program's only edge into stdlib.
	if isParamDefaultHelperName(name) 'aMintedDefaultHelper'
		markEveryMembersOwnDefaultHelper(name, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		return
	end 'aMintedDefaultHelper'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE PROVISIONAL-CALLEE RULE, AND IT HAS A SECOND READER ONE SCREEN DOWN
	// (`nameReachesStdlib`).** A call op carries the overload SET's BASE name until
	// `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` rebinds it to a member, so a base names EVERY member. This
	// side MARKS them; the short-circuit side TESTS them against stdlib. The rule is stated once, HERE,
	// and asked in the two shapes Maxon cannot fold into one — there is no way to hand a function the
	// action to take per member, which is the argument `Parser.surfaceMemberNames` makes about its arms.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **ONLY THIS SIDE WIDENED UNTIL X-array-retire, AND THE OTHER SIDE'S MISS WAS A PANIC.** For most
	// sets the narrow reader is accidentally right, because the FIRST declaration KEEPS the bare spelling
	// and the base therefore IS a function name (`String.contains`). For a CONTESTED extension method
	// **nobody keeps the bare spelling** (`Parser.overloadRegistrationNameFor`) — `Array.contains` is
	// declared by `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `where Element is Equatable` extension AND published onto `Array`
	// by `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable`, so its members register as
	// `Array.contains#type parameter` / `Array.contains#struct` and NOTHING is named `Array.contains`.
	// See `nameReachesStdlib` for what that cost.
	//
	// ⚠ **`contains` BEFORE `get`, AND THE HONEST STATUS OF THAT IS: NOT MEASURED TO MATTER HERE.** The
	// reasoning is that this runs once per CALL EDGE and a miss is what nearly every edge is, so a
	// `try map.get(k) otherwise return` would be an error construction per edge on the common path. That
	// reasoning is why it is spelled this way — and why the reader below spells it the same. **It is NOT
	// why the ladder moved**: it was X-contains's first hypothesis for a measured +0.101 allocations per
	// source byte, it was built, and re-running the ladder showed **not one allocation changed at any of
	// the six rungs** — the real term was `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` (see `maxonOpCalleeKind`).
	// Kept because a `contains`/`get` pair cannot be worse than a throwing `get` and it lets the miss be a
	// `panic` rather than a silent `return`; recorded this way because a comment claiming a measurement its
	// own instrument refuted is exactly the false prose this project keeps finding.
	if not project.overloadSets.contains(name) 'notAnOverloadSet'
		return
	end 'notAnOverloadSet'

	let members = try project.overloadSets.get(name) otherwise panic("markReachable: overloadSets lost the key '{String.from(name)}' between `contains` and `get`")
	for member in members 'eachMember'
		markOneReachable(member, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachMember'
end 'markReachable'

// One provisional helper name, widened over the callee's overload set — the member half of `markReachable`'s
// helper rule, split out so the two widenings read as the two they are rather than one nested pair.
//
// The callee it defaults is recovered through `paramDefaultCalleeSpelling` and the member's own helper minted
// through `paramDefaultHelperNameOnMember`, both at the mint, because a name derived a second time is what
// that mint's header records a backend `call to unknown function` for. A name the set does not hold — every
// helper of every unoverloaded callee, which is nearly all of them — widens to nothing and costs one map
// miss, the accounting the base widening below is written for.
//
// `markOneReachable` and not `markReachable`: a member's own helper is the name a declaration published, so
// it stands for itself and there is nothing further to widen.
function markEveryMembersOwnDefaultHelper(helperName ByteArray, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	let callee = paramDefaultCalleeSpelling(helperName)
	if not project.overloadSets.contains(callee) 'theCalleeIsNotOverloaded'
		return
	end 'theCalleeIsNotOverloaded'

	let members = try project.overloadSets.get(callee) otherwise panic("markEveryMembersOwnDefaultHelper: overloadSets lost the key '{String.from(callee)}' between `contains` and `get`")
	for member in members 'eachMember'
		markOneReachable(paramDefaultHelperNameOnMember(helperName, member: member), byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachMember'
end 'markEveryMembersOwnDefaultHelper'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES `name` REACH A STDLIB FUNCTION? — the SHORT-CIRCUIT's half of `markReachable`'s
// provisional-callee rule, and the half that did not exist (X-array-retire).** A name reaches stdlib
// either because it IS a stdlib function, or because it is an overload set's BASE and a MEMBER is.
//
// ⛔⛔ **WITHOUT THE SECOND CLAUSE A CONTESTED EXTENSION METHOD'S CALL WAS INVISIBLE, AND THAT WAS A
// PANIC RATHER THAN A SLOWDOWN.** `userCodeReachesStdlib` decides *"no op in user code names a stdlib
// function ⇒ file every stdlib name `unreachable`"*, and `lowerMaxonToStd`, `insertRangeChecks`,
// `scanRuntimeUsage` and `checkCalls` all skip what that set names. A program whose ONLY edge into
// stdlib was `nums.contains([20, 30])` — whose members are `Array.contains#struct` /
// `Array.contains#type parameter`, with nothing named `Array.contains` — therefore took the
// short-circuit, and `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` then reached the
// member the resolver had bound and PANICKED. That guard is the only thing that stood between this and
// an EMPTY function the program calls, with no diagnostic anywhere.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED, and the measurement is what identified the SHORT-CIRCUIT rather than the walk**: adding
// one non-overloaded stdlib call (`"a".isAscii()`) to the same program made it compile, because that
// edge is visible to this scan and the precise walk's widening then covered the members.
//
// ⛔ **AND THE TWO PROGRAMS THAT MEASURED IT ARE `DISABLED` IN `stdlib-whitelist.md`, WHICH IS THE HONEST
// STATE RATHER THAN A GAP LEFT OPEN.** `Array.contains` is today the ONLY contested
// `<Conformer>.<method>` in the corpus, and it did NOT retire from the `Array` roster — so with the
// roster serving it, those programs no longer cross into stdlib and would pass without exercising this
// rule at all. That file's trailer carries the red measurement and the reason; the cases go green with
// `contains`.
//
// ⚠ Over-answering is SAFE here and under-answering is the bug — a false TRUE costs the exact walk,
// where a false FALSE skips a body the back end calls. `contains` before `get` for `markReachable`'s
// measured reason.
function nameReachesStdlib(name ByteArray, project Project, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	if stdlib.contains(name) 'aStdlibFunction'
		return true
	end 'aStdlibFunction'

	if not project.overloadSets.contains(name) 'notAnOverloadSet'
		return false
	end 'notAnOverloadSet'

	let members = try project.overloadSets.get(name) otherwise panic("nameReachesStdlib: overloadSets lost the key '{String.from(name)}' between `contains` and `get`")
	for member in members 'eachMember'
		if stdlib.contains(member) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachMember'

	return false
end 'nameReachesStdlib'

// Mark exactly ONE function name, with no overload widening — the body `markReachable` shares between the
// name it was handed and each member of that name's overload set. Split out so the widening is written
// once and cannot recurse into itself (a member's own registration name keys no set).
function markOneReachable(name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	let idx = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	let already = try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")
	if already 'alreadyReached'
		return
	end 'alreadyReached'

	try reached.set(idx, value: true) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.set OOB at function index {idx}")
	names.insert(name)
	worklist.push(idx)
end 'markOneReachable'

// Walk `block.opRefs` DIRECTLY — not `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which allocates a fresh op-index array
// per function. This runs at the Maxon tier BEFORE any block-rebuilding pass, so `opRefs` holds no
// orphan ops (the concern that forces `liveOpIndices` at the Std tier), which is exactly why
// `scanRuntimeUsage` walks the same way here. A terminator names no callee, so visiting only the body
// ops in `opRefs` is complete for reachability.
//
// ⚠ **IT ALSO ANSWERED "DID ANY OP HERE THREAD A WITNESS TABLE?" UNTIL W129, RIDING ALONG ON THE
// `maxonOpCalleeKind` CLASSIFICATION THIS LOOP ALREADY COMPUTES.** That question had exactly one subject —
// `__cs_contains`, the last compiler-owned callee taking witness tables as ordinary arguments — and it went
// with the `__cs_*` runtime. **A new such callee brings the question back HERE and not into a second pass**:
// a walk of its own would re-derive, on every compile, a callee name this one already holds. See
// `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`'s header for the two routes that remain.
function markMaxonCallees(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, func IrFunction, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			markMaxonCalleeEdge(op, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			markLazyStaticInitCallees(op, project: project, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'markMaxonCallees'

// The static reachability edge one `MaxonOp` names, if any — asked of the DIALECT'S OWN classifier, which
// is the single place the op set answers "does this variant bind a callee name, and which operand is it?".
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS USED TO BE A THIRD COPY OF THAT TABLE, AND ITS STATED REASON DIED IN THE SAME COMMIT THAT
// LEFT IT STANDING** (BATCH2 slice 3 review). It re-derived the classification with its own band ranges,
// justified by `maxonOpCallee` allocating a union box per ask — a cost this slice's own optimizer pass
// removed by making the classifier a SCALAR enum (`maxonOpCalleeKind`). With the cost gone the copy is
// only a second answer: a variant appended to `MaxonOp` does still fail to compile at both sites (the
// band-append invariant), but nothing makes the two sites classify it the SAME WAY, and an
// under-approximation here is not a compile error — it is `lowerMaxonToStd` skipping a body that is then
// reached, which is exactly the `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panic this slice was opened by.
//
// A `functionValue` target is credited alongside a `direct` callee: a `functionRef`'s target is reachable
// code even though no `call` op names it. `indirectCall` calls through the code POINTER that `functionRef`
// already credited, and `maxonOpCalleeKind` answers `noCallee` for it.
function markMaxonCalleeEdge(op MaxonOp, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct to functionValue then markReachable(maxonOpCalleeName(op), project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		noCallee then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'
end 'markMaxonCalleeEdge'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE DATA EDGE THIS WALK CANNOT SEE — A WITNESS TABLE'S METHOD SLOTS — CREDITED AT THE CONFORMER
// THE WALK REACHES (W35).** `__witness_<Type>.<Iface>`'s slots are `.rdata` relocations, so the
// implementation each one names is named by no `call` anywhere in the module. Without this, an interface
// method whose body calls a stdlib function leaves that function filed `unreachable` while
// `DeadFunctionElimination` — which DOES follow those relocations — reaches it, and the two derivations
// disagree exactly as `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` says.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE KEY IS THE CONFORMER, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT NARROW.** `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable`
// has THREE callers and none of them is one op shape: an existential WIDENING at a call argument
// (`appendExistentialWitnessArgs`) or at a RETURN (`witnessForExistentialValue`), and a `where T is Iface`
// CONSTRAINT witness (`materializeWitnessArg`). Rather than predict all three from signatures, this asks the
// question they all share — **a witness table for `T` can only be built if a `T` VALUE EXISTS, and a `T`
// value can only be produced by a function OF `T`.** A type cannot be constructed from outside its own
// methods (E3076), so reaching some `T.<method>` is a precondition for every one of the three, and it is a
// precondition this walk already computes.
//
// ⇒ Reaching any function of a conforming type credits THAT type's requirement impls, and no others.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE FIRST TWO CUTS WERE BOTH WRONG, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND BOTH ARE WORTH KEEPING HERE.**
//   • **TOO NARROW:** keyed on a `witnessDispatch` OP. It fixed the dispatched case and left the other two
//     `ensureWitnessTable` callers panicking — a `Thing` boxed into an interface-typed field and never
//     dispatched, and a `where T is Digest` method that never touches `T`. Both compile on the merge base,
//     so the reachable-program set REGRESSED.
//   • **TOO BROAD:** keyed on a signature predicate, crediting EVERY conformance in the program. MEASURED
//     on one interface-boxing program, same binary both ways: allocations **370,345 ⇒ 671,836 (+81%)**,
//     bytes +20.9 MB, and the emitted code moved **3,388 ⇒ 3,788** — because `project.conformances` holds
//     every LISTED STDLIB module's conformances too, so `URL.equals`/`FilePath.hash` and their whole files'
//     bodies were demanded by a program that names neither. That is the blanket-seed trap
//     `seedLazyStaticInitCalleesOfUserCode`'s header records at 2223 moved fragments, by a second road.
//   ⇒ The conformer key is what makes it exact: a program that never reaches `URL` credits nothing of it.
//
// ⚠ **IT JOINS `<Type>.<requirement>` RATHER THAN READING `project.witnessSlotImpls`, AND A CUT THAT READ
// THAT MAP WAS WRONG. MEASURED:** the map is filled by `checkConformance`, which runs AFTER `queryAllModule`
// — and `growStdlibBodyDemand`'s fixpoint, which decides which stdlib bodies are BUILT AT ALL, runs INSIDE
// it. So the map is empty for every round of the fixpoint and full only by the time `deriveStdlibFacts`
// asks: the panic went away (the facts were right) and the emitted `@print` was an EMPTY function the
// program called — a silent wrong answer in place of a loud one, which is strictly worse.
// `conformances`/`interfaces` are merge-time stores (`Queries.resetMergeTargets` resets all three
// together), so they answer the same in both askers, which is the property this walk's header demands.
// ⇒ The `<Type>.<method>` join `ensureWitnessTable` is forbidden to make (R10) is correct HERE for the
// reason it is wrong there: a name no module function carries is silently skipped by `markReachable`, so a
// join that names nothing costs nothing, where a RELOCATION that names nothing is a wrong code pointer.
// Overloads are covered because `markReachable` credits the whole set.
//
// ⚠ **A CONTAINER'S KEY WITNESS NEEDS NOTHING EXTRA HERE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** Its
// conformer is the container's KEY, and a USER type there is
// refused: *"E2015: `Set` hashes and compares its keys through their `Hashable`/`Equatable` witnesses, so a
// key must be one of `int`, `String`, `Character`, `Array` — a 'Point' key is a later slice"*. So every such
// conformer is a BUILTIN whose impls `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` SYNTHESIZES, and a synthesized body calls no
// stdlib source, so nothing behind that table can be filed unreachable. The day user-type keys land, the
// conformer key above already covers them — reaching `Point.create` to put one in a `Set` credits
// `Point.hash` — which is a third reason to key on the conformer rather than on the call sites.
// ⚠ **THE KEY USED TO ARRIVE BY AN `ensureWitnessTable` ROUTE OF ITS OWN** — `withWitnessTableArgs`, hidden
// trailing arguments into the compiler-owned `__set_*`/`__map_*`/`__cs_contains` entries — and that route is
// gone (W105, W129): a corpus container's key witness is an ordinary dictionary parameter now, so the
// premise below arrives at the CONSTRAINT route rather than at a container one. The premise itself is
// unchanged, and it is
// load-bearing for the constraint route in its own right, which this walk's header states as P2
// with the measurement (`Keyed with String` under `where T is Hashable` builds `__witness_String.Hashable`
// while no `String.<method>` is ever reached). Read it as one premise with two customers, not two.
//
// ⚠ **THE PRICE IT DOES PAY, MEASURED AND KEPT HERE RATHER THAN IN A REPORT.** Reaching a conformer is
// weaker than boxing one, so a program that merely USES a conforming stdlib type credits impls it can never
// jump to: a `Directory`/`FilePath` program pays `FilePath`'s four requirement impls though it widens
// nothing — **+15,444 allocations (+1.1%), emitted code size UNCHANGED, and 11 committed fragments permute
// an `.rdata` label INDEX** (`__str_blob_44` ⇒ `__str_blob_54` and the like: more stdlib bodies are lowered,
// so the pool numbers differently). Verified meaning-free the only way a spec can verify it — the
// behavioural assertions in those same fragments still pass (`--filter=filepath` 40/0, `directory` 61/0,
// `url` 47/0), so no `lea` came to name a different string. The narrower gate that would remove this cost
// is named in this walk's header; it was weighed and not taken.
// ⚠⚠ **IT CREDITS EVERY CONFORMANCE A REACHED TYPE DECLARES — EXCEPT FOR THE SEED'S OWN CONFORMERS, WHICH
// ARE CREDITED PER NAMED INTERFACE INSTEAD (W188/W189).** The two routes answer from different evidence and
// the split is exactly where that evidence runs out:
//
//   • **A type the walk REACHED A FUNCTION OF** is evidence about the TYPE and none about which interface a
//     table will be built for, so every conformance it declares is credited. That total credit is what covers
//     the shape the interface key CANNOT see: an interface implied by a construct rather than named by a
//     declaration. **MEASURED (W189): a `where S is Iterable` at a `Vector with 3 Int` names `Iterable` and
//     NOTHING ELSE, while the loop it lowers to calls `Iterator`'s methods on the `ArrayIterator` the corpus
//     `createIterator` returns** — so keying `ArrayIterator` on the named set alone left `ArrayIterator.current`
//     unmarked and `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panicked on it.
//   • **A SEED conformer is one no reached function can ever belong to** (that is its membership rule), so
//     the total credit has nothing to fire on and the seed supplies the crediting instead — keyed on the
//     interfaces the reached code names, which is what keeps `float.toString` and its decimal bignum cone out
//     of a program whose only constraint is `Hashable` (the +76% image this pair of rules exists to prevent).
//     Crediting them HERE as well would undo exactly that: the seed marks `int.hash`, the drain reaches it,
//     and a total credit on `int` pulls in every other impl the type declares.
function markConformerWitnessImpls(func IrFunction, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, seedConformers ByteArrayArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match splitQualifiedName(func.name) 'owner'
		// A FREE function belongs to no type, so reaching it produces no conformer and implies no table.
		notQualified then break 'owner'
		parts(typeName, _) then markEveryDeclaredWitnessImplOf(typeName, project: project, signatures: signatures, seedConformers: seedConformers, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'owner'
end 'markConformerWitnessImpls'

// The total credit above, for a type the seed does not own.
function markEveryDeclaredWitnessImplOf(typeName ByteArray, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, seedConformers ByteArrayArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	if namesInclude(seedConformers, name: typeName) 'theSeedOwnsThisConformer'
		return
	end 'theSeedOwnsThisConformer'

	let conformedTo = try declaredConformanceIndex(project).get(typeName) otherwise 'declaresNoConformance'
		return
	end 'declaresNoConformance'

	for interfaceName in conformedTo 'eachInterface'
		markWitnessImplsOfConformer(typeName, interfaceName: interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachInterface'
end 'markEveryDeclaredWitnessImplOf'

// Credit every requirement implementation `typeName`'s witness tables can name. A type that declares no
// conformance has no table and nothing to credit, which is almost every type in almost every program.
//
// ⚠ The index is `declaredConformanceIndex`'s, not a second walk of `project.conformances`: that memo is
// reset in lockstep with the store it derives from (`Queries.resetMergeTargets`), so it cannot answer this
// walk from a previous merge round's declarations.
// ⚠ **IT TAKES BOTH COORDINATES — the conformer AND the interface — which is `ensureWitnessTable`'s own
// argument list (W188).** It used to take the conformer alone and walk `declaredConformanceIndex`'s answer
// for it, which is *"everything this type promises"*; a table is built for an interface the reached code can
// NAME, and it is the pair that decides which slots exist. See `reachableMaxonFunctionNames`' fixpoint for
// where the two lists come from and what asking them separately measured.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE CONFORMANCE TEST IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION — WITHOUT IT THE WALK MINTS NAMES NO DECLARATION COULD
// WEAR, AND `markReachable` DOES MORE THAN LOOK ONE UP.** It also widens a callee to its minted
// parameter-default helper, so a crossed pair like (`File`, `Hashable`) — a type that declares no `hash` —
// reached `splitMintedHelperName('__paramDefault#File.hash')` and **PANICKED on 8 cases**
// (`file-io`, `file-info`, `bytearray-element-size`). *"A name nothing declares is simply not found"* was
// true of `markOneReachable` and false of its caller. `typeConformsTo` is the right gate rather than a bare
// membership test because it walks `extends` and admits an INTRINSIC conformance — the two ways a slot can
// exist without the type's own declaration naming this interface.
function markWitnessImplsOfConformer(typeName ByteArray, interfaceName ByteArray, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	if not typeConformsTo(declaredConformanceIndex(project), interfaces: project.interfaces, typeName: typeName, interfaceName: interfaceName, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(typeName)) 'thisPairHasNoTable'
		return
	end 'thisPairHasNoTable'

	let iface = try project.interfaces.get(interfaceName) otherwise 'noSuchInterface'
		// A witness position naming an interface the program does not declare is E3017's / E3011's, reported
		// one pass later; a reachability walk has nothing to add and nothing to credit.
		return
	end 'noSuchInterface'

	// The TRANSITIVE requirement set, `ensureWitnessTable`'s own list — `iface.methods` alone would miss
	// every `extends`-inherited slot, which is a slot like any other. It is also what covers the `extends`
	// case the narrower gate has to get right: a `Base`-typed place against a `T implements Derived` builds
	// `__witness_T.Base`, whose slots are BASE's, and those are what this marks.
	for slot in interfaceWitnessSlots(project.interfaces, iface: iface) 'eachSlot'
		markReachable(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: slot.method.name), project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachSlot'
end 'markWitnessImplsOfConformer'

// ⚠⚠⚠ **A SECOND SEED, `markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime`, STOOD HERE AND WENT AT W129 WITH ITS LAST
// SUBJECT.** It rooted the corpus segmenter and the corpus `Set.contains` for the SYNTHESIZED Std bodies that
// call them — bodies appended after the pipeline, whose calls are therefore not edges of the MAXON call graph
// this walk reads. Its term list shrank to one (`__cs_contains`) and then to none. Two things it paid for are
// kept, because they are what the NEXT synthesized body calling corpus code must not re-learn:
//
//   * ⛔ **SEED AT THE REACHED OP, NEVER BY A PRE-PASS OVER THE MODULE.** The first version scanned every
//     function of the merged module up front — which includes STDLIB's OWN bodies, and a listed module calls
//     `.codepoints()`. So the seed fired on EVERY program: `return 1 + 1` lowered the whole corpus segmenter,
//     registered its eleven panic literals in `.rdata`, and shifted `__str_blob_0` to `__str_blob_11` in
//     **2223 committed fragments** of programs containing no string at all. (DFE then dropped the code, so it
//     was numbering and lowering work, never emitted bytes — which is exactly why a golden count was the only
//     thing that could see it.) It is `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s own trap — *"stdlib is internally connected,
//     so scanning its bodies would find an intra-stdlib edge on EVERY compile"* — and skipping stdlib bodies
//     would NOT have been the fix: a REACHED stdlib body naming such an entry needs the seed as much as a user
//     body does. Asked at the op, inside the walk, it is exact for both.
//   * ⛔ **SHEDDING A TERM EARLY IS THE FAILURE MODE.** A corpus function the seed named would be filed
//     unreachable, `skipUnreachedFunctionBody` would leave a bare `retVoid` behind, and a synthesized body's
//     call would land on it. A term goes only when nothing can emit its callee any more — which is the
//     evidence W129 carried for the last one: the `__cs_*` runtime is deleted, so no module can name it.
//
// The seed below is the one that remains, over the bodies the reachability walk is not the authority for.
// Stdlib's own bodies are skipped for `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s reason and for `liveGlobalLabels`': an access
// inside an unreachable stdlib body decides nothing, because that body reaches no back end and its slot is
// not kept for it.
function seedLazyStaticInitCalleesOfUserCode(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("seedLazyStaticInitCalleesOfUserCode: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("seedLazyStaticInitCalleesOfUserCode: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				markLazyStaticInitCallees(op, project: project, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'seedLazyStaticInitCalleesOfUserCode'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE OTHER EDGE THIS WALK CANNOT SEE — A LAZY STATIC'S INITIALIZER — CREDITED AT THE ACCESS THAT
// FIRES IT (W115).** `installManagedGlobalInit` synthesizes `__lazy_init$<label>` AFTER this walk, so the
// call from the access site to the initializer exists in no op the walk can read, and the functions the
// initializer calls are named by nothing in the module. What the walk CAN see is the `globalAddr` on that
// slot — which is precisely the access, since a lazy slot is read through no other op — so the edge is
// credited there. Exact in both directions rather than approximated: reaching the access is reaching the
// init, and not reaching it is not.
//
// ⛔ **THE ALTERNATIVE WAS TO ROOT THEM BESIDE `main`, WHICH IS WHAT `globalInitCallees` USED TO DO AND WHAT
// W115 MEASURED.** See that function's header for the two symptoms — an allocator and an orphan `.rdata`
// blob in every program in the language, the same pair W17 repaired for the witness seed. A root is a claim
// that the code runs unconditionally, and a lazy static's initializer is the one initializer that does not.
//
// ⚠ **OVER-CREDITING IS THE SAFE DIRECTION AND THIS DOES IT ONCE:** an access the program never executes
// still credits the callees, whose bodies are then lowered and dropped by dead-function elimination. A
// MISSING root is `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` — the standing warning the retired synthesized-runtime
// seed carried, kept in this function's own header block one screen up.
function markLazyStaticInitCallees(op MaxonOp, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	if not maxonOpNamesAGlobalLabel(op) 'namesNoSlot'
		return
	end 'namesNoSlot'

	for callee in signatures.lazyStaticInitCalleesOf(maxonOpGlobalLabelName(op)) 'eachCallee'
		markReachable(callee, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachCallee'
end 'markLazyStaticInitCallees'

// True iff any live op in USER code names a STDLIB callee, or a TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER does — the
// allocation-free pre-scan that lets a program which never touches stdlib skip the reachability walk and
// the substrate fixpoint entirely. It asks the same dialect classifier `markMaxonCalleeEdge` does
// (`maxonOpCalleeKind`), and differs from it only in what it does with a hit: it runs on every compile, so
// it must allocate nothing (no map, no queue) — it only asks "is one present?".
//
// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS OVER THE SAME LISTS THE PRECISE WALK ROOTS FROM — those shared lists are the guarantee,
// not a matching root SET**, and the difference is worth stating because the two are easy to confuse.
// `markReachable` widens a root over `project.overloadSets` and seeds the grapheme entries per reached op;
// this asks only "is this bare name a stdlib name?". Those agree EXACTLY, and here is why: a USER callee's
// own body is scanned by the loop below whatever it goes on to call, a STDLIB callee's bare name is the
// first overload member's registration name and so is always in `stdlib`, and an undeclared one is refused
// by the evaluator before it can reach either walk. An under-answer here would file ALL of stdlib
// unreachable for a program that reaches it — the direction that empties a body the back end then calls —
// so a new ROOT KIND owes that argument again rather than inheriting it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND ONE ARRIVED WITHOUT PAYING IT.** W17 rooted the corpus-defined intrinsic conformance impls in
// the precise walk and not here, so a program that took the short-circuit filed `String.hash` unreachable
// while its witness table still named it. It is now a MARKING on both sides, driven by the pair of
// predicates this scan asks below and the drain asks of reached code — see `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`,
// which carries the argument in full and the measurement of both failures.
function userCodeReachesStdlib(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet, initCallees ByteArrayArray) returns bool
	// ⭐ **A ROOT THE PRECISE WALK SEEDS IS A CROSSING NO BODY CARRIES, WHICH IS WHY IT CANNOT BE FOUND BY THE
	// OP SCAN BELOW.** `var sep = FilePath.separator()` reaches stdlib without any function of this module
	// naming it — the call belongs to `__module_init`, which does not exist yet. Missing it would answer "all
	// of stdlib is unreachable" for a program that reaches it, the direction that skips a body the back end
	// then calls.
	if anyRootNamesStdlib(initCallees, project: project, stdlib: stdlib) 'rootedInStdlib'
		return true
	end 'rootedInStdlib'

	// ⭐⭐ **AND A WITNESS TABLE IS THE OTHER SUCH CROSSING: its slots reach `String.hash`/`Character.hash`/
	// `Array.hash` through an `.rdata` RELOCATION, which is an op nowhere.** So this asks the pair of demand
	// predicates the drain asks of reached code, over USER code — exact for the same reason the stdlib-callee
	// scan below is: with no user edge into stdlib, the reached set IS a subset of user code. Gated on the
	// corpus actually DECLARING one of the seed's conformances, so an unlisted declaring module leaves this
	// scan — and every program's answer — exactly as it was.
	if corpusDeclaresASeededConformance(project, signatures: signatures) and userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib) 'widensOrThreadsOne'
		return true
	end 'widensOrThreadsOne'

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// ⭐ SKIP STDLIB's OWN BODIES. Stdlib is internally connected (`Clock.elapsedMs` calls
		// `Clock.nowMs`), so scanning its bodies would find an intra-stdlib edge on EVERY compile and
		// defeat this short-circuit. The exact question is "does USER code cross INTO stdlib?", and the
		// first edge into stdlib on any path from `main` is made by a USER caller — `main` itself is never
		// stdlib — so scanning only user bodies is the exact condition. (A stdlib-only reference is
		// unreachable-from-`main` anyway, so a false negative here is impossible; a false positive would
		// merely trigger the exact full walk.)
		//
		// It reads the set being BUILT rather than `isStdlibFunction`, which reads the PUBLISHED one:
		// this runs inside the derivation, before `project.stdlibFacts` holds anything.
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		// Direct `block.opRefs` walk — allocation-free, and safe for the same reason `markMaxonCallees`
		// and `scanRuntimeUsage` use it at this tier (pre-pipeline: no orphan ops; callees are body ops).
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("userCodeReachesStdlib: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("userCodeReachesStdlib: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				// ⚠ A THIRD term stood here — an op naming a SYNTHESIZED body that in turn calls corpus code —
				// and it went at W129 with `markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime`, whose roster it shared. The
				// PAIRING is the invariant, not the term: this short-circuit must answer TRUE for every edge that
				// seed roots, or the precise walk it skips would have rooted a name this scan called unreachable.
				if opNamesStdlibCallee(op, project: project, stdlib: stdlib) or opAccessesALazyStaticRootedInStdlib(op, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib) 'found'
					return true
				end 'found'
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return false
end 'userCodeReachesStdlib'

// The short-circuit's half of the witness-table demand — the SAME predicate the drain asks of reached
// code (`functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`), asked of USER code. It is a separate
// loop from the stdlib-callee scan above and deliberately so: that scan returns the moment it finds an edge
// into stdlib, so folding this in would make the answer depend on which of two independent facts a program
// happens to state first.
//
// It over-answers only by ignoring reachability — a user function nothing calls still counts — which is the
// direction that costs a walk rather than a wrong answer.
//
// ⚠ **AN OP-BORNE HALF STOOD BESIDE THE DECLARATION HALF AND WENT AT W129** with the last compiler-owned
// callee that threaded witness tables (see `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`'s header for the two routes
// that remain). The pairing with the drain is what matters and is unchanged: **this scan and
// `drainReachableWorklist` must ask the SAME predicates**, or the short-circuit would skip a walk that would
// have found a demand — the defect W17 measured, arriving from the other side.
function userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// Stdlib's own declarations are skipped for `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s reason: a demand inside a stdlib
		// body is reached only through a user edge into stdlib, which that scan has already answered TRUE for.
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		if functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition(func, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: project.funcSignatures) 'widensOne'
			return true
		end 'widensOne'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return false
end 'userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable'

// Does the CORPUS declare a conformance on a conformer the seed exists for — i.e. is there a corpus body a
// witness slot could name at all? Without a declaring module listed, these conformers conform only
// INTRINSICALLY (`isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`) and `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` synthesizes every impl, so
// no witness table can reach stdlib source and the demand question above must not disturb a single program's
// answer. Read off `declaredConformanceIndex` — the memo `markWitnessImplsOfConformer` marks through — rather
// than off a second reading of `project.conformances`, so the gate and the marking cannot disagree about
// whether there is anything to credit.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS THE SEED'S OWN LIST, AND IT SPELLED `String`/`Character` ITSELF UNTIL THIS RUNG — WHICH IS THE
// SHORT-CIRCUIT HALF OF EXACTLY THE DEFECT THE SEED REPAIRS.** `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s header records the
// obligation in so many words: a root kind added to the precise walk alone lets a program whose user code
// names no stdlib callee take the short-circuit and file the impl `unreachable` while its witness table still
// names it. A conformer added to the seed and not here would be that omission wearing a gate's clothes.
//
// ⚠ **NO PROGRAM'S ANSWER MOVES TODAY, and saying so is the point rather than a caveat.** `stdlib/String.maxon`
// and `stdlib/Character.maxon` are listed UNCONDITIONALLY and each declares a conformance, so this gate is
// already TRUE in every compile and adding `Array` to the list it reads cannot change one. What the shared
// list buys is that the gate stays right for the reason it is written — the seed and its gate are ONE roster —
// rather than staying right by accident of which two modules happen to be unconditionally listed.
function corpusDeclaresASeededConformance(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns bool
	let index = declaredConformanceIndex(project)
	for conformerName in conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods(signatures) 'eachSeededConformer'
		if index.contains(conformerName) 'declaresOne'
			return true
		end 'declaresOne'
	end 'eachSeededConformer'
	return false
end 'corpusDeclaresASeededConformance'

// Does any name on the precise walk's initializer root LIST belong to stdlib? Kept a named function rather
// than inlined: a second root LIST would be one call, and cannot then arrive with a subtly different test.
function anyRootNamesStdlib(roots ByteArrayArray, project Project, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for name in roots 'eachRoot'
		if nameReachesStdlib(name, project: project, stdlib: stdlib) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachRoot'

	return false
end 'anyRootNamesStdlib'

// Whether one `MaxonOp` names a callee in `stdlib` — the SAME dialect classifier `markMaxonCalleeEdge`
// asks, over the same four callee-bearing variants.
//
// ⚠ Its header used to say it was *"a SEPARATE band match on purpose: it runs on every compile, so it
// must allocate nothing (no boxed callee union…)"*. That was true of `maxonOpCallee`; it is not true of
// `maxonOpCalleeKind`, which is a scalar enum and allocates nothing, and the NAME is fetched only for the
// two kinds that carry one — i.e. only where this function was going to read a callee anyway. The
// allocation-free property the comment defended is kept, and the second copy that was defending it is not.
function opNamesStdlibCallee(op MaxonOp, project Project, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	return match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct to functionValue gives nameReachesStdlib(maxonOpCalleeName(op), project: project, stdlib: stdlib)
		noCallee gives false
	end 'kind'
end 'opNamesStdlibCallee'

// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD CROSSING NO BODY CARRIES: A USER ACCESS OF A LAZY STATIC WHOSE INITIALIZER CALLS STDLIB
// (W115).** `type Cache / static let table = StrMap.create()` reaches `Map.create` through a
// `__lazy_init$` that `installManagedGlobalInit` has not synthesized yet, so the op scan above sees only the
// `globalAddr` — and answering "all of stdlib is unreachable" there is the direction that skips a body the
// back end then calls.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED PER USER OP, NEVER OF THE WHOLE ARENA, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE SHORT-CIRCUIT.** A
// LISTED module's lazy static is reachable only through an accessor of its own type — a stdlib call
// `opNamesStdlibCallee` already finds — so an arena-wide test would answer TRUE for every program in the
// language the day any listed module declares one. `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` declares eleven, so that is
// not hypothetical: it is the difference between `var flag = true` taking the allocation-free short-circuit
// and taking the full walk.
function opAccessesALazyStaticRootedInStdlib(op MaxonOp, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	if not maxonOpNamesAGlobalLabel(op) 'namesNoSlot'
		return false
	end 'namesNoSlot'

	return anyCalleeIsIn(signatures.lazyStaticInitCalleesOf(maxonOpGlobalLabelName(op)), stdlib: stdlib)
end 'opAccessesALazyStaticRootedInStdlib'

// The loop the caller above keeps out of its one-line answer. It reads the set being BUILT rather than
// `isStdlibFunction`, for `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s stated reason: this runs inside the derivation that
// publishes it.
function anyCalleeIsIn(callees ByteArrayArray, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for callee in callees 'eachCallee'
		if stdlib.contains(callee) 'crossesIntoStdlib'
			return true
		end 'crossesIntoStdlib'
	end 'eachCallee'

	return false
end 'anyCalleeIsIn'

// ⭐ **WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS NEED THE x64-WINDOWS SUBSTRATE, AND WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY EACH REACHES.**
// The fact a user's crossing call is blamed with, so the refusal names the stdlib function they wrote
// instead of a line inside `stdlib/`.
//
// ⭐ TRANSITIVE, because a stdlib module is written in LAYERS. `Clock.elapsedMs` names no runtime entry at
// all — it calls `Clock.nowMs`, which names `__gt_now_ns` — and a program calling `elapsedMs` must be
// refused at ITS call. So the requirement propagates BACKWARD along the stdlib call graph to a fixpoint:
// the seeds are the functions that name an entry directly, and a caller inherits its callee's.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE ENTRY NAME, NOT A BIT**, which is why it is not `CsrGraph.reverseReachabilityMark`:
// `Clock` alone reaches TWO entries (`__gt_now_ns` for the monotonic clock, `__clock_now_unix_s` for the
// calendar), so a bit would leave the diagnostic naming whichever one it guessed — a wrong answer, not a
// cosmetic one. What PROPAGATES is still a scalar: a node inherits its callee's SEED INDEX, and the names
// are read off `seedEntries` once at the end.
//
// COST: O(stdlib functions + their ops + their intra-stdlib edges), bounded by the stdlib source LOADED
// and not by the user program, and computed only for a compile that actually calls into stdlib. Each node
// enters the worklist at most once and each reverse edge is walked once, so it stays linear in the loaded
// stdlib as that grows from today's one module to all of it.
//
// The UNREACHABLE stdlib functions are left out entirely: they are invisible to this compile, so their
// substrate is too. That is what keeps a `Clock.nowMs()` in code `main` never calls compiling for wasm.
function stdlibSubstrateEntries(module MaxonModule, stdlibNames ByteArrayArray, unreachable FunctionNameSet) returns StdlibSubstrateEntryMap
	let nodeCount = stdlibNames.count()
	var nodeOf = StdlibNodeByName.create()
	for i in 0 upto nodeCount 'index'
		nodeOf.upsert(try stdlibNames.get(i) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: stdlibNames.get({i}) OOB below its own count()"), value: i)
	end 'index'

	var state = SubstrateFixpoint.overNodes(nodeCount)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'
		if let node = try nodeOf.get(func.name) 'stdlibFunction'
			for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
				let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
				for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
					let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
					noteSubstrateEdge(op, callerNode: node, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
				end 'eachOp'
			end 'eachBlock'
		end 'stdlibFunction'
	end 'eachFunc'

	state.propagateToCallers()

	var entries = StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create()
	for i in 0 upto nodeCount 'publish'
		if state.requiresSubstrate(i) 'needsIt'
			entries.upsert(try stdlibNames.get(i) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: stdlibNames.get({i}) OOB in publish"), value: state.entryOf(i))
		end 'needsIt'
	end 'publish'
	return entries
end 'stdlibSubstrateEntries'

// The mutable state of the substrate fixpoint over the stdlib call graph's DENSE node space. It is one
// object rather than six threaded columns because the body scan and the propagation write all of it, and a
// helper that took them apart could be handed five of the six.
//
// ⭐ THE COLUMNS PROPAGATE SCALARS. A marked node stores an INDEX into `seedEntries`, never the entry name
// itself: reading a name out of the answer while inserting into it is a read-borrow held across a mutation,
// which the ownership checker refuses (E3070) — correctly, since the insert may reallocate what the read
// borrowed. The names are read once, after the fixpoint has settled.
type SubstrateFixpoint
	// Per node: does it (transitively) need the Win32 substrate? Also the "already queued" test — a node is
	// marked exactly when it enters the worklist, so it enters at most once.
	var marked as BoolArray
	// Per MARKED node: the index into `seedEntries` of the seed it inherits its entry from.
	var seedOf as CsrColumn
	// The runtime entry each seed names, in the order the seeds were found.
	var seedEntries as ByteArrayArray
	// Nodes whose callers still have to inherit from them. Drained by index, never popped, so a node's
	// position is stable while the array grows.
	var worklist as CsrColumn
	// The intra-stdlib call graph, REVERSED as it is collected (callee -> caller), so the propagation
	// walks from a function that names an entry to the functions that reach it.
	var edgeCallee as CsrColumn
	var edgeCaller as CsrColumn

	static function overNodes(nodeCount CsrIndex) returns SubstrateFixpoint
		var marked = BoolArray.create()
		marked.resize(nodeCount)
		var seedOf = CsrColumn.create()
		seedOf.resize(nodeCount)
		return Self{marked: marked, seedOf: seedOf, seedEntries: ByteArrayArray.create(), worklist: CsrColumn.create(), edgeCallee: CsrColumn.create(), edgeCaller: CsrColumn.create()}
	end 'overNodes'

	// `node` names `entry` directly — a SEED.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS USED TO SAY "THE FIRST ENTRY A BODY NAMES IS THE ONE IT IS REPORTED FOR: a body naming two is
	// refused for either, AND BOTH ARE EQUALLY TRUE OF IT" — AND THAT STOPPED BEING TRUE WHEN E3074 LANDED.**
	// The entry now decides WHICH DIAGNOSTIC the crossing gets, not merely which name it quotes, so two
	// entries are two different claims with different advice. `SemanticCheck.substrateEntryOutranks` is the
	// one place that says which wins and why; here it is only obeyed. An entry that outranks the incumbent
	// REPLACES it and re-queues the node, so the callers that already inherited the weaker one are corrected.
	//
	// ⚠ The upgrade is MONOTONE — a rank only ever rises, and the rank has two levels — so a node is
	// re-queued at most once and the fixpoint below stays linear.
	function seed(node CsrIndex, entry ByteArray)
		let already = try self.marked.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.seed: marked.get({node}) outside the node space")
		if already and not substrateEntryOutranks(entry, incumbent: self.entryOf(node)) 'keepTheIncumbent'
			return
		end 'keepTheIncumbent'

		self.mark(node, seedIndex: self.seedEntries.count())
		self.seedEntries.push(entry)
	end 'seed'

	// A call edge inside stdlib, recorded reversed for the propagation below.
	function callEdge(calleeNode CsrIndex, callerNode CsrIndex)
		self.edgeCallee.push(calleeNode)
		self.edgeCaller.push(callerNode)
	end 'callEdge'

	// Mark `node` as needing the substrate `seedIndex` names, and queue its callers for the same.
	function mark(node CsrIndex, seedIndex CsrIndex)
		try self.marked.set(node, value: true) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.mark: marked.set({node}) outside the node space")
		try self.seedOf.set(node, value: seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.mark: seedOf.set({node}) outside the node space")
		self.worklist.push(node)
	end 'mark'

	// ⭐ THE FIXPOINT: every stdlib function that REACHES a seed over stdlib's own call edges inherits its
	// entry. A reverse BFS over the CSR adjacency — each node enters the worklist once (the marking IS the
	// visited test) and each edge is walked once, for O(nodes + edges).
	function propagateToCallers()
		let callersOf = buildCsr(self.edgeCallee, to: self.edgeCaller, nodeCount: self.marked.count())
		var head = 0 as CsrIndex
		while head < self.worklist.count() 'drain'
			let node = try self.worklist.get(head) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: worklist.get({head}) below its own count()")
			head = head + 1
			let seedIndex = try self.seedOf.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: seedOf.get({node}) outside the node space")
			for k in 0 upto callersOf.degree(node) 'eachCaller'
				let caller = callersOf.neighbour(node, k: k)
				if self.inheritsFrom(caller, seedIndex: seedIndex) 'newlyRequiredOrOutranked'
					self.mark(caller, seedIndex: seedIndex)
				end 'newlyRequiredOrOutranked'
			end 'eachCaller'
		end 'drain'
	end 'propagateToCallers'

	// Should `caller` take `seedIndex` as its own — because it has no entry yet, or because this one
	// OUTRANKS the entry it already inherited (`SemanticCheck.substrateEntryOutranks`)? An unmarked caller is
	// the ordinary case and answers before any name is read; the second question is asked only of a node that
	// is already marked, so the common propagation pays one bool test.
	//
	// ⚠ It is a question rather than an inlined `if` because the propagation and `seed` must ask the SAME one:
	// a seed that upgrades a node while its callers keep the weaker entry would leave the two halves of one
	// fixpoint disagreeing about which diagnostic a crossing earns.
	function inheritsFrom(caller CsrIndex, seedIndex CsrIndex) returns bool
		if not (try self.marked.get(caller) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.inheritsFrom: marked.get({caller}) outside the node space")) 'noEntryYet'
			return true
		end 'noEntryYet'

		return substrateEntryOutranks(try self.seedEntries.get(seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.inheritsFrom: seed {seedIndex} does not exist"), incumbent: self.entryOf(caller))
	end 'inheritsFrom'

	function requiresSubstrate(node CsrIndex) returns bool
		return try self.marked.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.requiresSubstrate: marked.get({node}) outside the node space")
	end 'requiresSubstrate'

	// The runtime entry `node` reaches. Defined only for a node `requiresSubstrate` accepts — an unmarked
	// node's `seedOf` slot was never written, and the panics say so rather than returning slot 0's name.
	function entryOf(node CsrIndex) returns ByteArray
		if not self.requiresSubstrate(node) 'unmarked'
			panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: node {node} needs no substrate")
		end 'unmarked'
		let seedIndex = try self.seedOf.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: seedOf.get({node}) outside the node space")
		return try self.seedEntries.get(seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: node {node} names seed {seedIndex}, which does not exist")
	end 'entryOf'
end 'SubstrateFixpoint'

// One stdlib body op's contribution to the substrate fixpoint: it either NAMES a substrate runtime entry
// (a seed) or calls another stdlib function (a reverse edge). A callee can never be both — the parser
// reserves the `__` prefix for compiler-owned names, so no stdlib declaration can carry one.
//
// The band structure — and the band-append protection — is `markMaxonCalleeEdge`'s, the third member of
// that family in this file. Each asks a different question of the SAME four callee-naming variants, and
// each pays a different cost (this one runs only for a compile that calls into stdlib), which is why they
// are separate matches; a variant appended past `witnessTryDispatch` fails to compile in all three rather
// than being silently swallowed by any of them.
function noteSubstrateEdge(op MaxonOp, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		literal to stringLiteral then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		throwError to panicAbort then break 'op'
		functionRef(_, funcName) then noteSubstrateCallee(funcName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		indirectCall then break 'op'
		asyncCall(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		await to witnessTryDispatch then break 'op'
		// A panic names no callee at all, so it reaches no stdlib substrate.
		panicAbortValue then break 'op'
		// The error-flag encode names no callee at all, so it reaches no stdlib substrate.
		errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
		// A `spawn` names a TYPE; the factory it calls is an ordinary `call` op beside it and is noted there.
		serviceSpawn then break 'op'
		// ⭐ A SEND REACHES ITS MESSAGE, so it is an EDGE exactly as an `async` spawn is — the handler runs on
		// another green thread, but it is the same function and it reaches whatever that function reaches.
		serviceSend(_, callee, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
	end 'op'
end 'noteSubstrateEdge'

// Classify ONE callee named by a stdlib body: a runtime entry that needs the Win32 substrate SEEDS the
// fixpoint, another stdlib function becomes a reverse edge, and anything else — a user callback, or a
// target-neutral runtime helper — carries no substrate requirement.
//
// ⭐ `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` is asked, never restated: it is the ONE place that knows which runtime
// entries only x64-windows can provide, and a second list here would be that fact written down twice.
function noteSubstrateCallee(callee ByteArray, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	if calleeNeedsWin32Substrate(callee) 'substrateEntry'
		state.seed(callerNode, entry: callee)
		return
	end 'substrateEntry'

	if let calleeNode = try nodeOf.get(callee) 'stdlibSibling'
		state.callEdge(calleeNode, callerNode: callerNode)
	end 'stdlibSibling'
end 'noteSubstrateCallee'
